div> </div>Where on Mars? It can get below -200 (farenheight) at the poles.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T20:42:21-07:00","id":567289,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=567286#post567286\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where on Mars? It can get below -200 (farenheight) at the poles.</div> </div> </div> </div>It was sunny side, not the poles. I think they were detecting something like -45 C on the Martian surface and Edmonton was sitting at a lovely -60 C at time. So yes, it was an abnormally cold day for Earth and an abnormally warm on for Mars, but still. <br /> <br /> Actually, doing a Court System based around extreme weather would be cool, especially since I at least don't usually think of Canada's weather as being extreme, although living in Abu Dhabi has kinda helped to correct that notion. Calgary could easily have Courts of Hail, Tornado, Blizzard, Chinook, and Flood.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T23:48:10-07:00","id":567474,"title":"","content":"Here's the last of my Gold Mountain Courts, the <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1OsM4g57txqb0QCaKJDbK4HxPai91jICslB84IC39ZnI/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Court of the Tower</a>. They're all pretty rough, obviously, but I'm happy to have drafts ready for when second edition hits. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Speaking of extreme-weather courts, I think it might be interesting to come up with a two court system for regions in the tropics that have very distinct dry and wet seasons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 06-25-2015, 02:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-25T18:00:34-07:00","id":568559,"title":"","content":"This document has been giving me ideas for a coastal freehold based around the tides. Arcadia is like the ocean: mercurial, inimical to human life, but full of wonders and riches. The Tidal Courts are founded on a system of respect for the ebb and flow of Faerie's connections to this mortal realm.<br /> High Tide: The Court of Bounty, the Diving Pelican, home to treasure hunters, provisioners, and fishermen (Emotion: Satiety)<br /> Low Tide: The Court of Famine, the Burrowing Crab, home to ascetics, warriors, and scouts (Emotion: Hunger)<br /> Flood Tide: The Court of Disaster, the Ruinous Leech, home to EMTs, survivalists, and parasites (Emotion: Alarm)<br /> Rip Tide: The Court of Depths, the Lurking Monster, home to thieves, killers, and mystics (Emotion: Fear)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-25T19:05:37-07:00","id":568652,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MachineIV</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=567208#post567208\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel spoiled. I have &quot;OMG beautiful snow, also there are monkeys&quot; then &quot;OMG cherry blossoms make everything pink&quot; then &quot;hot but the rain is wonderful and makes up for it&quot; then &quot;the mountains turn red and orange and persimmons are amazing&quot;. I basically live in a picture book.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The iconic and beautiful representation of all four seasons in Japan actually makes me think the Seasonal Courts should be pretty well represented there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-25T19:12:40-07:00","id":568658,"title":"","content":"I was thinking about theoretically adapting the various gamelines to the Requiem for Rome era. So now I'm considering what the Roman Courts of the era would be. First edition at least tied the Seasonal Courts back to Ancient Rome, but its not like the seasons are super-pronounced there. I was considering several rival Courts each based on different cultural foundation myths (perhaps seven, each named for and headquartered upon one of the seven hills) - and despite the great animosity and conflicting agendas between them, the Bulwark they mantain is the Pax Romana. If any Courtier is slain by a Courtier of a different Court, the offending Court has a fortnight to bring their member to justice, or the Pax is forfeit and the Fata (True Fae) and their Nemeses (Hunstmen) have free reign.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T05:05:00-07:00","id":569043,"title":"","content":"Great Idea with Pax Romana Courts, <b>Data</b> - it's also logical, as Roman Empire is known from it's eclecticism. And Pax Romana is<b> very Roman</b> story to explore. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Besides the Seasonal Courts, what other groups you would see in Ancient Rome?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T05:10:10-07:00","id":569047,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=568652#post568652\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> The iconic and beautiful representation of all four seasons in Japan actually makes me think the Seasonal Courts should be pretty well represented there.</div> </div> </div> </div>But would not Japan have it's own Courts until the Edo Period? Seasonal Courts, as Roman invention, would come with European changelings with European traders in XVI century, I guess.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T07:14:49-07:00","id":569165,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=569047#post569047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But would not Japan have it's own Courts until the Edo Period? Seasonal Courts, as Roman invention, would come with European changelings with European traders in XVI century, I guess.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that'll be as much the case in Second Edition.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T07:37:19-07:00","id":569197,"title":"","content":"So, does anyone have any great inspiration for a Court System in the Iconic Town of Arkham, Massachusetts (of Lovecraft fame)? I am currently using the Seasonal Courts Structure (though further grouped into <i><b>Seelie (Spring/Summer) </b></i>and <i><b>Unseelie (Autumn/Winter) </b></i>classifications), and while pretty decent, they are not really grabbing me by the Mivonks and squeezing till I cry, like I would like them to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The problem with defending the purity of the English language is that English is about as pure as a cribhouse whore. We don't just borrow words; on occasion, English has pursued other languages down alleyways to beat them unconscious and rifle their pockets for new vocabulary. - James. D. Nicoll</span></span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","name":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T07:53:55-07:00","id":569202,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=569165#post569165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think that'll be as much the case in Second Edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. The changing of the seasons is so ubiquitous around much of the world. It would seem really weird to me if in all of history, only in Europe was it used as a foundation for a Courts system.<br /> <br /> More interesting questions to me are whether said other seasonal Court systems differed substantially,* whether many of them are still around, and what led the dominant form of Seasonal Courts to emerge into its evident international reach. Similar questions and situations apply to the Seelie/Unseelie Court system.<br /> <br /> * I imagine they would have to, myself. As we've seen in this thread, depending on where you are, a season can mean very different things. Spring, Summer, Autumn and Winter aren't going to translate to desire, wrath, fear and sorrow everywhere, say, and the associated ideals are likewise going to vary.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://avatarcomic.net/exalted-wiki/Exalted_3E:What_We_Know\" target=\"_blank\">The EX3 What We Know Wiki</a>, courtesy of Marin and JMobius<br /> <a href=\"http://nishkriya.com\" target=\"_blank\">Nishkriya, the EX3 Freelancer Quotes Tracker</a>, courtesy of Anathema and Moonwolf<br /> <a href=\"http://forums.whitewolfarchive.com/\" target=\"_blank\">The White Wolf Forum Archive</a>, courtesy of The Dark Wizard and Sanctaphrax (see <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/PhilippeSaner?u=159709\" target=\"_blank\">its Patreon</a>) <hr />Arrghus: There's also the Shepherd of the North Star, busily guiding the one thing in all the heavens that <i>doesn't move</i>.","edited":{"userid":558,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/558-gaius","user":"Gaius","date":".  Must be consistent about 'Court' and 'Courts'! 06-26-2015, 11:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/558-gaius","name":"Gaius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T10:15:39-07:00","id":569369,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tymeaus Jalynsfein</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=569197#post569197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, does anyone have any great inspiration for a Court System in the Iconic Town of Arkham, Massachusetts (of Lovecraft fame)? I am currently using the Seasonal Courts Structure (though further grouped into <i><b>Seelie (Spring/Summer) </b></i>and <i><b>Unseelie (Autumn/Winter) </b></i>classifications), and while pretty decent, they are not really grabbing me by the Mivonks and squeezing till I cry, like I would like them to.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm... I'm not intimately familiar with Livecraft's Arkham, are there any local legends or folklore that could be used as a founding story for a Freehold?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T10:32:41-07:00","id":569379,"title":"","content":"Well... Hmmm... Nyarlahotep &quot;The Black Man&quot; figures in Arkham pretty heavily.<br /> I am currently going through the Cthuthlu Mythos to try and mine it for ideas, but it is slow going.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The problem with defending the purity of the English language is that English is about as pure as a cribhouse whore. We don't just borrow words; on occasion, English has pursued other languages down alleyways to beat them unconscious and rifle their pockets for new vocabulary. - James. D. Nicoll</span></span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","name":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T11:38:51-07:00","id":569457,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tymeaus Jalynsfein</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=569197#post569197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, does anyone have any great inspiration for a Court System in the Iconic Town of Arkham, Massachusetts (of Lovecraft fame)? I am currently using the Seasonal Courts Structure (though further grouped into <i><b>Seelie (Spring/Summer) </b></i>and <i><b>Unseelie (Autumn/Winter) </b></i>classifications), and while pretty decent, they are not really grabbing me by the Mivonks and squeezing till I cry, like I would like them to.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well...<br /> <br /> First of all, it's important to realize that in HP Lovecraft's Arkham, assuming you want to base your setting on the original source material, all of the terrible, supernatural goings-on generally go unnoticed by its citizenry and the world at large. Whereas Innsmouth is literally run by a sinister cult and is plainly a weird and sinister place to outsiders who stay there for any length of time, the people of Arkham are mostly perfectly normal New Englanders. Although there are various dark rumors surrounding the place, it's primarily a college town, and Miskatonic University is presented as a highly respected academic institution... which just happens to have a rather interesting collection of rare books.<br /> <br /> One big theme, then, could be the duality between the apparent normalcy of the place, and the hideous secrets that it conceals. Perhaps you could have an autumn-like court that deliberately delves into terrifying occult secrets, but another cult that's dedicated to reclaiming a normal human existence.<br /> <br /> Another potential theme is <i>decay</i>. Lovecraft seems to have had something of a phobia regarding aging and old things in general, the most obvious expression of which is his referring to horrible godlike aliens as &quot;old ones&quot; or &quot;elder gods.&quot; This is a common feature of the way he describes places as well: many of his characters are antiquarians, and bad things usually happen in old houses or decaying towns past their prime. Parts of Arkham could exemplify this; according to the Encyclopedia Cthulhiana, Arkham used to be a thriving seaport, and formerly was home to a fair amount of industry, but in time the sea trade failed and the mills mostly shut down following a major flood and a cholera outbreak in the late 19th century. In spite of the economic stimulus provided by the University, there could still be a fair number of economically depressed neighborhoods full of moldering, abandoned buildings dating back two hundred years or more.<br /> <br /> <br /> Overall, I'd say that the seasonal courts are probably a decent fit, if you spin them right. Both the Spring and Autumn Courts could be based around Miskatonic University, with the former reveling in the vibrant campus culture and the latter delving into secrets man was not meant to know with the aid of the famous Miskatonic library (and perhaps their own &quot;special collection&quot;). The Summer Court might take on an unusually academic bent as well, patronizing college sporting events and taking on roles like Dr. Henry Armitage from &quot;The Dunwitch Horror&quot; or the protagonist from &quot;The Case of Charles Dexter Ward,&quot; men of learning who use unconventional methods to combat supernatural threats.<br /> <br /> The Winter Court, on the other hand, might hide out among the poorer sections of the city, feeding on the general despair and doing their best to avoid the strange goings-on in the town, and at the University in particular.<br /> <br /> <br /> In keeping with Lovecraft's fiction, the Gentry familiar to Arkham's Changelings would probably be particularly alien and unknowable. Some might have an obvious interest in humanity, like Nyarlathotep in many of his guises, but others might seem entirely unaware of the lives they destroy, coming and going without any clear rhyme or reason and sometimes dragging people back to their incomprehensible nightmare realms seemingly by accident or as an afterthought. The Huntsmen who serve them wouldn't necessarily understand them any better than Changelings or humans do, and might be more like deranged cultists, attempting to serve their masters' unknowable desires out of fear. These strange Gentry may or may not actually care about getting their captives back, but the Huntsmen have a largely irrational belief that they can win favor with their &quot;gods&quot; by offering up Changelings as &quot;sacrifices.&quot;<br /> <br /> One notable characteristic of Nyarlathotep in particular is that he's often described as the &quot;soul and messenger of the Outer Gods.&quot; It may be that he's simply another title and guise of a more alien and aloof Keeper, who enters the world to ensnare Changelings and bring them before his bizarre alter-ego for reasons that make no sense to anyone but him.<br /> <br /> Another figure that might be particularly appropriate for a Changeling game is Cthulhu, whose influence on the world is based on his ability to manipulate the dreams of certain individuals around the world. He could be one of the Gentry who takes on the form of a sleeping alien giant in Arcadia, but also invades the dreams of sleeping mortals and Changelings, driving them to madness and perhaps also into the hedge, where his capering Huntsmen-cultists can capture them and drag them down to dread R'lyeh under the black seas of Faerie.<br /> <br /> <br /> You might also take a look at Lovecraft's Dreamlands stories. They're more fantasy than horror, and there's an argument to be made that they're mostly inferior imitations of Lord Dunsany's style, but they have a somewhat fairly talish feel to them and involve all sorts of strange places and people that could be parts of Faerie.<br /> <br /> Speaking of which, Lord Dunsany is awesome (if very much a product of his time), and great inspiration for Changeling. Plus, all his stuff is in the public domain. Look him up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3209,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","user":"CatDoom","date":". 06-26-2015, 02:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T13:04:45-07:00","id":569506,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tymeaus Jalynsfein</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=569197#post569197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, does anyone have any great inspiration for a Court System in the Iconic Town of Arkham, Massachusetts (of Lovecraft fame)? I am currently using the Seasonal Courts Structure (though further grouped into <i><b>Seelie (Spring/Summer) </b></i>and <i><b>Unseelie (Autumn/Winter) </b></i>classifications), and while pretty decent, they are not really grabbing me by the Mivonks and squeezing till I cry, like I would like them to.</div> </div> </div> </div> Besides the awesome stuff CatDoom suggested, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/168334-arkham-lost\">Atamajakki wrote some stuff on the idea.</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-26T16:03:58-07:00","id":569715,"title":"","content":"When I have time, I am going to write up a nomadic Freehold based on the story of the Great American Road Trip. Now, just have to find that time...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-28T15:04:17-07:00","id":571924,"title":"","content":"Players are going for the Rose Courts. I am full of win.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-28T19:34:42-07:00","id":572106,"title":"","content":"<b><u>La Maison du Regret</u></b><b><u>-The House of Regret and the Birth of Paris's Bulwark</u></b><br /> <br /> In 1862, during the construction of the grand opera house the Palais Garnier, workers digging out foundations ran into the problem of flooding. The site for the building had unusually high groundwater. Wells and pumps were installed, and yet the water kept coming in. Finally, the workers decided to use the water to their advantage, designing a cistern that would fill up until the water pressure made it equal, keeping it steady. The opera house would house their props, costumes, and set pieces down there in time. And to this day, the water cannot be removed.<br /> <br /> Maybe it was waiting to fulfill it's purpose.<br /> <br /> Paris,traditionally, has been a proud sponsor of the Seasonal Courts for ages, and took pride in how they brought together the courts into the freehold system around the time of the Sun King. World War II changed all of that when Germany invaded France, with Nazi Lost taking advantage of their knowledge of the freehold and breaking their Bulwark. French Resistance spy and former Spring Courtier Nicolette Chaput rallied the Lost of the time and quickly developed the Community of the Teeth-and-Forest Threshold, a freehold of hard lessons and sacrifice built off of the story of The Grandmother's Tale, making sacrifices every so often and taking advantage of their hard knock lessons. During the war, it worked-the Parisian Lost sacrificed their enemies and traitors alike with each defeat, and hit as hard as they could without being found out. When the war ended, though, the courts were on edge as their means of turning the True Fae away began to demand more from their members. The Community threatened to devour itself in its' adolescence.<br /> <br /> A shift occurred in 1946 when Chaput met the Belleroses, a changeling couple that fled to America before the war. Marcel Bellerose was a historian, and in fact studied much about the Court of the Sun King and the advent of freeholds during his reign, and proposed a dueling system that would allow the war-torn Lost an outlet for the traumas they picked up from the resistance as well as bringing in the old grandeur of the Sun King to reinvigorate the spirit of the Parisian Lost. Diane Bellerose was a film critic and an early fan of Jean Cocteau's Beauty and the Beast, and she grafted that story to Little Red Riding Hood to create the Cycle of the Maiden, Wild One, and Woodsman, softening the demands and broadening the symbol base the Wyrd had to work with. Together, the three of them constructed the Bulwark and laid the groundwork for the courts, and prepared for the sealing of the ritual.<br /> <br /> At the start of spring, the three of them snuck down to the cistern of the Palais Garneir. No one really knows if it was just out of convenience for secrecy, or if the three had divined a secret from the water of the opera house. Diane Bellerose filled the role of the Maiden, Nicolette Chaput was the Wild One, and Marcel Bellerose served as the Woodsman. The intent was not only to begin the cycle that would defend Paris, but allow Chaput to retire her role as a leader of Paris and quietly slip off into retirement. Almost as though by the hand of ravenous fate, the ritual was instead consecrated with the brutal stabbing of Marcel, his body tossed into the cistern and greedily taken by the deep darkness. The closeness of Diane and Nicolette from then on spoke volumes of the story and true intent, though the details were never revealed.<br /> <br /> The Bulwark of the Cycle has never needed to be as bloody since, but changeling occultists since have wondered whether or not the act defiled the House of Regret ever since.<br /> <br /> The House of Regret is comprised of three places: The Palais Garnier in Paris, an abandoned house called the Wrecked Hut(Cabane Naufrage) in the Hedge, and a 'dream bastion'* called The Water Between(L'eau Entre) that connects the two by the cisterns in both of their basements. It's called a dream bastion because that's what it most closely resembles, but as it stands, no one is really quite sure what the Water Between actually is-anyone can float through and breathe and wander the waters as easily as a dream. The three can be traversed by anyone as much as any Hedge Gate, but rarely is it used this way.<br /> <br /> This is because the House reacts strangely to creatures of the Wyrd. It brings haunting visions and realities that dredge up loss and trauma, damaging moments for the ruins of a fae's mind. On days other than the ritual days, a changeling in either the opera house or cabin suffers a Clarity 1 Breaking Point every hour, though this can be avoided for a day by being invited in by the Wayward-after the day is up, the invited cannot receive the benefit again for a number of days equal to the Wayward's Mantle. Mortals and other supernatural creatures witnessing the visions that plague Wyrd creatures suffer related integrity loss as is pertinent to their respective scores.<br /> <br /> In the Water Between, the free-flowing and ill-defined space of sorrow that it is, a changeling suffers the same breaking point every ten minutes, and mortals suffer an Integrity 0 Breaking point at the same points in duration(Vampires, Mages, Mummies, and Demons suffer the same for their respective stats. Werewolves suffer the next breaking point towards Spirit every hour. Prometheans risk Pilgrimage 1 breaking points every hour, but can be averted for a day by earning a point of Pilgrimage from their time in the water. Sin-Eaters, curiously enough, do not suffer Synergy losses of any kind, though they and their Geists are noticeably shaken by the experience, and suffer the Shaken Condition during their time and afterwards as a result. Beasts are treated as Satiety 10 within the Water and seem to think they have every right to be scared shitless by that place, similarly suffering the Shaken Condition).<br /> <br /> Hobgoblins and True Fae will not enter any part of the House without invitation from the Wayward, and Huntsmen prefer to avoid it, suffering a penalty to their actions equal to the Wayward's Mantle within the House unless invited by the Wayward or earning a Yearning off of the Wayward.<br /> <br /> On ritual days, changelings who are friends of the courts(Status Freehold, Court Goodwill or Mantle 1) can enter the Palais Garnier or the Wrecked Hut without fear of the House's retribution, but that doesn't mean it stops warping reality and perspective. Instead, the House's illusions and dreams set the stage and prepare in anticipation of the ritual performance, namely in providing the Final Gate that each new Wayward is pushed through upon failing the ritual. These illusions do not cause Clarity or Integrity loss unless a particular elements related to the character is evoked in the spirit of the evening(ie, the story would include iconography and staging that would hit on individual breaking points for a character) The Water Between remains as grim as ever, though. No one knows what will happen to the House if the Bulwark fails. Waywards who have kept the House don't know any better what would happen, but always seem inclined towards fatalistic outcomes.<br /> <br /> It is one of the most potent mystical landmarks within France, but only in the presence of the Wyrd. It is the only place the Parisian Lost would enact their Bulwark, seemingly a central point for the strange powers of the fae that brings the rituals to life. Bridge-burners claim it's an egg for an Arcadian Realm. Necromantically inclined fae suggested it is touched by the Underworld. Lord Sages of the Unknown Reaches sometimes wonder if it connects to the Shadow or the Astral. It is the Rose Court's greatest achievement and most terrifying secret, and it seemingly only loosely answers to one individual.<br /> <br /> The Wayward.<br /> <br /> More on her later, I think.<br /> <br /> *I call in a dream bastion to denote a sort of physical place that operates like dreams, rather than the Bastions founds in Swords at Dawn. It's a thing I use in my games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 08-21-2015, 04:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-28T20:16:54-07:00","id":572119,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tymeaus Jalynsfein</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=569197#post569197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, does anyone have any great inspiration for a Court System in the Iconic Town of Arkham, Massachusetts (of Lovecraft fame)? I am currently using the Seasonal Courts Structure (though further grouped into <i><b>Seelie (Spring/Summer) </b></i>and <i><b>Unseelie (Autumn/Winter) </b></i>classifications), and while pretty decent, they are not really grabbing me by the Mivonks and squeezing till I cry, like I would like them to.</div> </div> </div> </div>forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/168334-arkham-lost<br /> <br /> Done and done.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-28T22:11:34-07:00","id":572204,"title":"","content":"I think I have the rough order of operations from here on the Paris writeup.<br /> <br /> 1) The Wayward<br /> <br /> 2) The Bulwark, the Roles, and The Married<br /> <br /> 3) The Sovereigns, Crowns and Mantles, and the Choir of the Stars<br /> <br /> 4) The Duels and The Courted<br /> <br /> 5) So, What Does the Freehold do Beyond the Story?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 06-29-2015, 01:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T11:05:23-07:00","id":572920,"title":"","content":"For random background purposes, Nicolette was an Ogre, Marcel was a Fairest, and Diane was probably a Wizened. We'll have to see.<br /> <br /> <b><u>The Wayward</u></b><br /> <br /> The Wayward was never an essential role. Sure, in Beauty and the Beast, the story is very much about a daughter shifting her affection for her father onto another man, and his stealing the Beast's rose is what prompted the story. Sure, the grandmother needed bread and milk. But the story doesn't hinge on them, and quite often they either get written out or killed-sometimes subsequently eaten. They serve as impetus for the Maiden to enter the Forest, and they often serve as a tool of transition for her, but they are not an actor in the dynamics of the story. As such, the Bulwark isn't ruined if there is no Wayward. The role is supplementary, a convenience. At it's core, the only reason the Wayward is preferred is because the alternative is a body of corpses.<br /> <br /> Of course, the Wyrd recognizes the Wayward as so much more than a victim or a vain plot point. The Wayward is the one who dwells at and brings forth the Threshold, a creature half in light and shadow, an incarnate of both the familiar and the foreign. So, much like anything else in faerie culture, the Wayward is a mixed bag, a curse mixed in with some blessings.<br /> <br /> The Wayward is chosen with the completion of the Cycle's Bulwark. When the Maiden, Wild One and Woodsman duel to determine which one of them is to 'die', it is sanctified by the wounding of the one acted against, whether it comes a result of a fight or the conclusion of a long and rational discussion. This wound must be at one lethal damage. The first curse of the Wayward are her wounds-they don't heal for the next nine months, when the ritual is performed again. They don't get progressively worse, but a character's health is effectively capped by the damage. Few forces under the moon and the sun can heal this damage, and the consequences of defying the Wyrd on this precedent are unknown.<br /> <br /> The Bulwark is not properly completed until the Wayward proceeds through the Water Between to the opposite side of the House of Regret(Palais Garnier to Cabane Naufrage or visa versa, based on which location she was wounded in). The Wayward stumbles throughout the opera house or cabin as the House focuses it's dreamscapes solely on her, creating a descent into a personal hell of loss until she makes it out of the Water Between. The second and third curses come through the passage of the Water. First, the Wayward suffers a Clarity drop, reducing her current Clarity by 1. Secondly, she ages to a proportionate equivalent of ten years for a mortal(So if a changeling can live up to 200 years, she ages 20, so on and so forth). The House takes it's toll.<br /> <br /> From then on till the next commencement of the Bulwark, the Wayward is banned from the City of Paris and it's equivalent Hedge territory. The Cabane Naufrage is a rare Hedge Locale in that is not a fixed point like other Hollows and settlements within the Hedge, always being at least a six-hour journey away from the furthermost Hedge settlement that belongs to the Parisian Lost. Hedge cartographers often use the Cabane as the measuring stick in relation to the fixed hollows to define territorial spaces, much to the consternation of the Hedge inhabitants. But for the Wayward, these boundaries are important-The Hedge actively closes up at her approach into the Parisian Territory, routing her away from it. Should she try to come in the mortal world, the flow of the Wyrd manipulates events and conspires to keep her out. One way or another, if she enters Paris, she will either be stopped or pay for it.<br /> <br /> The exception to this rule is the Palais Garnier, and here is where the blessings begin. The Wayward is welcome in the House of Regret, and treated as though she belongs in all ways. No mortals question her presence in the opera house, and people who meet her for the first time in the House start with a Good Impression of her for social maneuvering uness she goes out of her way to make a bad impression. She may join in the theater troupes, mingle with the elite who come for shows, and in general never faces trouble that may cause her to be rejected or removed from the premises. The presence of a Wayward often ensures that the Lost can have the place open to them on ritual nights. The Wayward's connection to the House means she can dip into it's well of regret at any time, harvesting the emotion at will, and so effectively also has infinite Glamour while in the House-something no other fae may do. Finally, the House does not shift in response to the Wayward's Wyrd, and cannot be the cause of any further Clarity Breaking Points for the Wayward while she bears the role.[I'm debating about whether the House absolves her from Clarity Breaking points that her role might otherwise force her into]<br /> <br /> The Water Between still plagues her with visions of regret, but in the closest thing to an act of kindness the House ever does, the Water emphasizes things the Wayward regrets that the Wayward can do something about when her time is up. Retiring Waywards often know exactly what they are going to do when their time is up thanks to this.<br /> <br /> The Wayward may invite people in for up to a day and spare them from the effects of the House on their Clarity. She may put as many stipulations on her invitation as she likes, and if accepted, the invited must maintain them. Changelings, mortals, and other supernatural creatures suffer the normal effects of the House the instant they break her rules. Hobgoblins and True Fae who break the rules of the invitation are filled with dread at the oncoming sorrow, and can take no action that isn't directly related to leaving the House until they are out.<br /> <br /> Huntsmen can accept the invitation, and should they break it, they must focus on leaving. They can take other actions on the way out, but they take a penalty to their actions equal to the Wayward's Mantle, with each action that is not related to leaving after the first increasing the penalty by 1(So if the Wayward has a Mantle of 3, the Huntsmen would take a -3 penalty to their first action not related to leaving, -4 to their second, etc.). Huntsmen can also avoid the House's curses by gaining a Yearning off of the Wayward, which gives him access to the House without penalty for a number of hours equal to the Wayward's Mantle.<br /> <br /> Once the day is over, the particular invited members cannot be allowed back in for a number of days equal to the Wayward's Mantle. If a invited member leaves before the day is up, it counts as a visit and as such must wait the allotted period.<br /> <br /> The Wayward also gets +3 dice to traversing the Hedge and finding things within it. Asides from making them adept Hedge explorers, this makes them invaluable in defense, since they can use this blessing to find approaching enemies and use their curse in relation to the Hedge to block off the ways in. Of course, this puts them at risk of being right in the line of fire.<br /> <br /> Despite the potency of the Role, changelings who are cast for the Final Duel do not envy the losses and vulnerabilites the Wayward faces, or the isolation of the Freehold. The Bulwark must be maintained, and such a life if preferable to killing, but many would rather win, even if the blessings of the Union were not there, than be cast into the deathly archetype.<br /> <br /> *For the record, I realize there's a nasty stereotype beginning to develop, this idea that marriage is must-have goal for people and that people who don't or for some reason can't should kill themselves. This is one of the reasons the Wayward role exists rather than simply dying-a character cast as the Wayward can struggle to prove there is more to life beyond that. But also I wanted a system that is flawed and archaic in some ways, so as to keep a door open for characters with revolutionary aspirations. The Wayward, in this way, is also the rallying point for every person who feels outcast by the system and it's tendency to typecast people in terms of the roles, and can very well lead the charge to break the system.<br /> <br /> <b><u>NEXT TIME: The Bulwark of Paris and the Married, Champions of the Cycle</u></b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 08-21-2015, 04:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T11:40:18-07:00","id":572974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ZealousChristian24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=569506#post569506\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Besides the awesome stuff CatDoom suggested, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/168334-arkham-lost\">Atamajakki wrote some stuff on the idea.</a></div> </div> </div> </div>Awesome stuff <i><b>Catdoom</b></i>... Thanks <i><b>ZealousChristian</b></i>, as well, for <i><b>Atamajaki's </b></i>Link.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The problem with defending the purity of the English language is that English is about as pure as a cribhouse whore. We don't just borrow words; on occasion, English has pursued other languages down alleyways to beat them unconscious and rifle their pockets for new vocabulary. - James. D. Nicoll</span></span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","name":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T13:13:35-07:00","id":573070,"title":"","content":"Question to the room: each Union formed from the Final Duel not only offers the usual benefit of adding your Mantle against the True Fae, but a bonus to certain skill rolls, two for each kind of Union. Should this benefit be +1 or +2?<br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> <br /> <b><u>Sidebar: Into the Depths</u></b><br /> <br /> Why on earth would anyone even bother going into the House of Regret asides from ritual days? Sure, it can be used for defensive purposes for a limited amount of time, but asides from that, the consequences for anyone going into the House are so sharp, you have to wonder who would risk it. A night at the opera is not worth the Clarity.<br /> <br /> The answer lies in its' strangely real properties as a physically shifting place of loss. The House of Regret does sometimes merely cause hallucinations, but it also sometimes is manifestly real, and it draws it's tools and images all the way from a person's own unique mythology to the broad archetypes of their regrets that have been around from the dawn of time. While that last aspect might allow for personal recovery tools, the more tempting aspect is simply this: anything that is lost can theoretically be found there. From the stuffed animal lost as a child to some of the most potent Tokens ever to slip the mind of the True Fae, the simple truth is that a changeling willing to brave the House, particularly the Water Between, can profit greatly from it.<br /> <br /> This isn't a highly advised course of action. THe House is not just waiting to spill out spoils-its' nature is to drown a person in their own emotion. But for those willing to take the hit by spending the time, they may find what they need. Some of Paris's greatest treasures have come from the House.<br /> <br /> Curiously, this is one thing a Wayward <i>cannot </i>do in their time minding the House. They will come to know the House and it's oddities more intimately than anyone else, but a Wayward will generally take nothing with them but experience.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 06-29-2015, 07:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T17:59:31-07:00","id":573370,"title":"","content":"<b><u>The Bulwark of Paris, the Final Duel, and the Married</u></b><br /> <br /> At it's simplest form, the Bulwark for the Cycle of the Maiden, Wild One, and Woodsman is superficially not complicated. Every nine months, three people representing the three roles gather together in the House of Regret and either literally kill one of three, or metaphorically kill them by wounding them so they bleed and having them pass through the Water Between. It's really that simple...in theory.<br /> <br /> SIDEBAR TIME!<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><b><u>Can the Wayward Screw Up the Bulwark?</u></b><br /> The loser of the Final Duel is not properly the Wayward until they have crossed through the Water Between after being wounded. Killing the Wayward on the way to the Water just completes the ritual, but if the Wayward is delayed until the day has passed, the Bulwark breaks, the Wayward loses the first curse, and Paris is vulnerable for nine months.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> In execution, the Bulwark is actually very demanding. The ritual demands <i>sincerity</i>-sincerity in how much a person embodies the role, sincerity in the motives for the sacred matrimony and murder. The people doing this are not just acting out a play, they are making the ancient tale alive again, bringing age old conflicts and lessons up again and again. That means the people involved need to have stakes in the performance of the ritual. The ritual has been failed before*, and they mark some of the darkest times for Paris since World War II.<br /> <br /> In game terms, each role must be filled by someone who meets the following list. First, they must be a friend of the Paris Freehold at the least, expressed by Mantle, Court Goodwill, or Status(Freehold) 1. Second, they must have each of the traits described for the role in each Court-these traits have been divined from experimentation with the House of Regret and what it seems count as passing. The usual way of confirming this is by having a participant successfully win a duel against a member of each court. Third, the participant must also exhibit a final trait that the house deems defining for the roles.<br /> <br /> This final requirement is there to allow freedom to the Storyteller to set the tone for their stories. The House shifts it's opinions on this, leaving an edge of uncertainty to the entire state of affairs to fuel conflict. The Storyteller should have something in mind rather than leave it as an arbitrary decision.<br /> <br /> SIDEBAR TIME!<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><b><u>What is Actually Needed?</u></b><br /> <br /> You may have noticed some caginess to the phrasing above. Do Changelings have to be the participants? Are the Duels required?<br /> <br /> Strictly speaking, the answer to both of those is &quot;No, but it helps.&quot;<br /> <br /> In regards to the Participants, not one of them <i>has</i> to be a changeling, so long as they have Court Goodwill or Freehold Status and traits regardless. It could be a mortal, a Beast, a Vampire, even a True Fae or Huntsman who has somehow obtained these requirements, though unlikely. That said, a changeling must be present at the execution of the Bulwark to trigger the House as the judge of the sanctity of the event, and few creatures are quite as in tune with the demands of the Wyrd as faeries. Still, if you're running a crossover game and you want your pretty little werewolf to be the Maiden, go for it.<br /> <br /> In regards to the Duels, the Duels are semi-mystical rituals themselves that do tie into the Bulwark, but as a refinement process that was added on after the fact rather than a core feature. A character can just be approved of by a member of each Court for the role and be good, or even just inherently possess the traits to make them ideal for the role. It's <i>rare</i>, but it can happen that a character is a perfect fit without aid.<br /> <br /> Does that affect the execution of the Duels? Sort of, but not by a whole lot. The Sovereigns keep their eyes on the Sphere Score(more on that later), and if they find people who happen upon these aspects, they tend to prefer keeping them in reserve and using the Duels to make primary candidates anyways. If the worst should happen and a participant be kept from their performance in anyway, the Sovereigns like to have cards no one had eyes on to quickly fill the roles to keep the Bulwark going in times of crisis.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Similarly, the Final Duel, whether it manifests as a fight or a deep-seated discussion with a vote at the end, needs to be performed with sincerity. The two who 'kill' the third have to believe, for whatever reasons, that this person needs to go based on the merits of their own defense. The politics that get involved make this tricky, as participants toeing a party line can ruin the ritual. There's no mechanical way to measure this, though a stop gap might be that each person must spend a point of Willpower in regards to their determining conflict against the person they think should be 'killed'.<br /> <br /> The Final Duel is treated as though the participants were in a dream, with each one using their highest Power, Finesse, and Resistance Attribute for their rolls, although their Wyrd does not count as a equipment bonus.***<br /> <br /> Beyond the basics, anything added is pagentry-and the fae do love their shows. While the simple version can be done at any time the ritual is required, the Parisian Freehold tends to make a night of it, arranging the reservation of the Palais Garnier for the evening and allowing mingling and eating and partying before and after the ritual. The Bulwark itself is made more theatrical in it's execution-not only because it brings the Lost together, but because the final play ensures a last shot at building sincerity before the Final Duel.<br /> <br /> The common script begins with the Maiden and the Woodsman on stage, taking time to interact with one another. The scene is cut short by the Wayward(or someone filling in if the last one died) calling for the Maiden, usually asking for help. The Maiden 'arrives' to find the Wild One, and these two take some time on stage to also engage one another, before the Wayward again calls the Maiden to come to her. The Maiden leaves, and the Woodsman arrives, confronting the Wild One for some time, before the Maiden returns and the Final Duel begins. The proper metaphorical killing includes the remaining two shoving the newly wounded Wayward through a massive pair of gates into the back of the stage, where she will wander her way down to the cistern and through the Water Between. The play on stage concludes with the Sovereigns rising from their seats and declaring the winners Married through their bloody Union.<br /> <br /> The time the participants spend on stage are spent engaging one another. They may argue and fight over personal affairs between them, or they may confront one another on their beliefs and the failings of them. It may be wholly intellectual and slightly staged, or it can be personal affair. No script is actually handed to them-sincerity must determine the dialogue and action.<br /> <br /> The fulfillment of the Bulwark imparts the usual benefit of every courtier receiving their Mantle as a defense bonus against the True Fae, but an additional benefit is bestowed on all members of the freehold based on the Union that is formed that lasts for the next nine months. A Union of Maiden and Wild One offers a +2 to Investigation and Persuasion. A Union of Maiden and Woodsman offers a +2 to Occult and Survival. A Union of Wild One and Woodsman offers a +2 to Brawl and Subterfuge. On top of this, the Courts represented by the Union(through Mantle or Goodwill) are declared the rulers of Paris for the next nine months, and have full access to their Crowns.<br /> <br /> As for the Married, the victors of the Final Duel are granted several privileges by Wyrd and community alike, though some aspects may be mixed blessings. The first and most immeadiate effect is that the two now benefit from an oath between the two of them. In game, the particular blessing is determined by the Wyrd, but players and Storytellers should devise an appropriate blessing for their relationship. Second, the Married are invited to the Council of the Sovereigns in an advisory capacity. There's not a lot of real power, as the role is similar to religious leaders, philosophers, and poets kept by political leaders today, but they still learn many secrets of the city(though not the Sphere Score) and can influence decisions in their capacity as a Voice for the Wyrd. Third, they gain 3 dots of Fame(Freehold), Barfly, and 4[/5?]**** Dots they can spread between Staff, Retainer, Contacts, Allies, and Resources for the next nine months. the 4 dots represent a shared benefit between the two of them, so they must handle that carefully, and their Fame and Barfly are not assured for after the nine months, though those can be maintained.<br /> <br /> [I want to have a special blessing for each based on their roles, but I'm having trouble gauging how much is too much. The ideas are that a Maiden can basically stop a fight, period, so many times, a Wild One can convince someone of anything so many times, and the Woodsman can find anyone so many times]<br /> <br /> The Married are not permitted to fill the roles of the next Ritual-the Wyrd rejects them outright. Many find that going one or two rounds before applying themselves again to the roles feels best, though some people certainly re-apply themselves again and again as much as possible. The Sovereigns usually dissuade people from doing this, following their own ineffable plans to ensure the safety and sanctity of Paris.<br /> <br /> <b><u>NEXT TIME: The Sovereigns and the Score of the Spheres</u></b><br /> <br /> *I know earlier I said it's never happened and that there would be consequences if the House was not fed the Bulwark Ritual. I figure some of that is a little lame for history purposes-changelings should know some of the consequences of failing. That said, the House has 'grown' over the years, and the consequences that accompany failure increase as the years go by. The House should be as terrifying as the True Fae, and I want to maintain that fear of it potentially incubating <i>something</i>.<br /> <br /> ***I intend to update this when we get the Oneiromancy rules. In the meantime, I'm not crazy, right? 1E's Oneiromachy had you use your highest traits, right?<br /> <br /> ****Is 4 enough?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 08-24-2015, 03:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T18:27:29-07:00","id":573385,"title":"","content":"Arc, that's looking excellent. You're putting as much nuance and flavor to everything as possible and I really like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T18:31:51-07:00","id":573412,"title":"","content":"Thanks! Things started really clicking after yesterday's chat with my players, and I'm kind of hopped up on the urge to write for it(which doesn't help, because I'm supposed to be making videos and websites, but whatever).<br /> <br /> There's some points where I wonder if I've gone too far or not far enough, but I think I've got the balance and direction of it more or less right. I hope that when I get it done and refined, this is a city people'll use(and yes, once it's at that point, I'll lay it out and see if I can art it up and release it as a PDF).<br /> <br /> In the mean time, let me know what you think needs more or less or some shoving sideways!<br /> <br /> EDIT: Alternatively, let me know if there are any particular elements you really like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 06-29-2015, 09:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T19:38:36-07:00","id":573478,"title":"","content":"When I'm less slumped down by my studies I'll try and look over the Paris Freehold more carefully! And point out the details I like the most.<br /> <br /> So many things to do, so little time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-29T22:56:19-07:00","id":573624,"title":"","content":"Updated the Bulwark. Barring the blessings of each role, everything's in there. I hope to get the Sovereigns out tomorrow. They're manipulative bastards, but they mean well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-30T21:08:56-07:00","id":574701,"title":"","content":"On a related note that's actually been swimming in my head for a while and just came up to the surface again:<br /> <br /> Whenever I remember Inheritance in Beast The Primordial, I wonder at the mileage a Begotten might bring to the stories that Changelings weave. And I think of the Beast Incarnate, a living legend of flesh and nightmares, as part of a Freehold's Story. Their Myth as part of the Bulwark, intimately tied to its workings. To Destabilize the Myth might endanger the Bulwark, as much as attacking the story itself could, and to shatter the Bulwark is to shake the Beast's Myth and potentially strip them of the power of their Inheritance.<br /> <br /> But the Incarnate is a monster to be feared indeed, or it would not be be such a pillar for the Freehold; and the Courts who support such a legend frightening on their own right.<br /> <br /> There is only room for one Villain in this story. Or the Beast is as though the land itself, a primordial being that extends its influence and protection to the Freehold under the tradition of ritual and praise. Maybe a maiden is sacrificed to the monster to placate it. Maybe the Begotten's Myth is some dreadful, terrifying guardian tyrant. Or perhaps the nightmare is not placated, but seduced. Perhaps it is conquered as part of an ancient oath, that its loss would bind it into a role of deadly keeper.<br /> <br /> The storytelling possibilities for it are delightful!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":188,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","user":"YeOfLittleFaith","date":". 07-01-2015, 12:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-30T21:55:44-07:00","id":574765,"title":"","content":"And there's my next freehold project.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-01T11:50:16-07:00","id":575415,"title":"","content":"No crowns or mantles today. Sorry guys, but mechanics is something I wanna think about(plus something I wanna chat with my players about). That saaaaaiiiid...<br /> <br /> <b><u>The Sovereigns of Paris and the Score of the Spheres</u></b><br /> <br /> While the groundwork for the courts was in progress when the Cycle's Bulwark was first enacted, they weren't completed for the first few performances. By the time they were, the expectation of the time was that the Married ruled the city of Paris. But mixing city politics with a ritual that demands sincerity became a problem, and one of the first in the handful of failures over the years came from the Engaged trying to perform the ritual to force a Union between Maiden and Woodsman in 1951 as changelings were dealing with the civil consequences of France going to war with Vietnam in the First Indochina War. The subsequent nine months were a disaster for the Parisian Lost as the Married and the failed Engaged tried to patch things up while the True Fae swept in and the Courts scrambled to arrange defenses, and despite the subsequent successful performance of the Bulwark, the populace declared something had to change. At this point, the Courts solidified as political entities, with each Court developing their own Sovereign who operated in a council for the maintenance of Paris's freehold. They were tasked with ensuring handling the management of the actual freehold, as well as seeing to the facilitation of the Bulwark's performance <i>without</i> influencing the people involved or the actual performance of it. They are to see that the Bulwark happens, but they cannot directly choose the people for it or the direction that it actually occurs in.<br /> <br /> This sort of detachment from the Bulwark was a new thing for the Parisian Lost, with the Monarchs of the Seasonal Courts and the Captains of the Teeth-and-Forest Community being directly involved with the Bulwark itself, and while cycle and sacrifice did force their hands, they at least were able to steer the course of their Wyrd-granted blessings. It was clear the same couldn't be done with the Sovereigns, and yet the needs of the city often times clashed with the needs of the Bulwark. Furthermore, the facilitation created some complex issues for the Sovereigns. The Duels were quickly elevated from a way to keep the population busy and bring back some pride to a way of determining who could fill the Roles, and yet the Sovereigns couldn't force the duels into being a required thing of the populace, a thing to keep on schedule-not only would that defy the sincerity demanded from the Bulwark, but it opened a can of worms of moral and ethical dilemmas that risked turning the freehold into a dystopia. On top of that, any form of government is considered ideal when the people are content and conflict is low, but the Duels were effective at finding people suited for the Roles despite being sparked by conflict in the Freehold.<br /> <br /> Sovereigns aren't idealist figures. The Courts can engineer their paragons, and the Bulwark <i>will</i> have its' pretty little icons. Sovereigns, on the other hand, very rarely have Mantle 5, and if they performed in the Roles, it's usually quite some time before they make the move for Sovereignty. They do strive to embody the ideals of their Courts-they have to, in order to win the trust of the courtiers- and they naturally have conflicts with one another as a result of following the differing psycho-spiritual philosophies. At the end of the day, though, the Sovereigns are more keenly aware of the practicalities of their positions, and are often more keen to work with one another in order to keep things running. The Sovereigns appoint the successors, and they look for practicality and flexibility alongside the virtues of their own Courts. A Sovereign who can't find a compromise is hardly a Sovereign at all. And so, the Sovereigns have devised a compromise with their circumstances and follow a secret creed: Contain the Conflict, Carve the Rivers of Fate.<br /> <br /> Their secret to the threefold problem of minimizing conflict, filling the Roles, and directing the performance of the Bulwark is divination and manipulation, plain and simple. Enter the Choir of the Stars and the Score of the Spheres.<br /> <br /> The Sovereigns employ a number of divination attempts, but they're cautious about employing people to do the divination for them given the sensitivity of what they do. Sovereigns who discover those with contracts and gifts of foresight watch and test them carefully, trying to weave them into a cast of seers loyal to the state. Those who they believe are willing to put the maintenance of Paris above all else are inducted into the secret council called the Choir of the Stars, there to sing Fate's songs, and chart them on the Score of the Spheres.<br /> <br /> The Score of the Spheres is the grand achievement of the Sovereigns over the years. A potent divination tool, the Score is a star chart the size of a large round table, overlaid by infinity music scores. Using it can grant a +5 to mundane and supernatural divination attempts. Divinations received by someone using the Score or someone who has an Expression Speciality(Music Scores)* or Occult Specialty(Astrology)* can be added to the score by marking up notes that align with the stars on the map-people who used the map inherently know where to place it, while specialists must roll Intelligence+Occult OR Expression+Wyrd to divine the right place to mark it. Each divination added puts a die to the Score's pool to be used for either of it's special features beyond it's use as a divination tool.<br /> <br /> Once a month, a Sovereign can use the Score to divine people with strong potential to fulfill the role and aspects about their life, using Wits+Occult+Wyrd+any amount of dice pulled from the Score's pool, with each success revealing either a person or an aspect of their life that strongly can be used to move them, such as their rivalry with the baker, their romance with their neighbor, or their passion to perform in the opera. Any Sovereign can divine as many people as they want and can afford on the day they start the first one, but must wait the month once that day is up.<br /> <br /> A Sovereign can use the Score to cast a Contract on or [REDACTED]** anyone who has either been divined by the Score or added to it, anywhere, for a scene. In order to use this, a die must be spent from the Score's pool, and the Sovereign must spend a point of Willpower. Dice can be pulled from the score to augment any use of this map or to pay the cost of Glamour.<br /> <br /> The Score clears itself with the completion of the Bulwark ritual, and constantly changes day to day, both in accordance to the shifting of the sky and in regards to the events of the city. The Zodiac shine prominently on it, and other constellations stars are constants, but there are also stars that never existed in the sky that appear on the Score. Divining what each star is can often mean a great deal for keeping Paris safe and prosperous.<br /> <br /> With their resources, their skills, the Choir and the Score, Sovereigns find ways to gently push more prominent potentials together in such a way as to force a Duel, and more importantly push those they think should win into place. It's a delicate balance, but the Sovereigns believe that they can lead the Wyrd down the rivers they carve using the Score to push for certain results without violating the results. It's not perfect-the Score and the Choir have failed to get all the details before, and for being a clearly magical item, no one has yet quite been able to discover the drawback to it. The Sphere Score seems to be bound to the Bulwark as much as anything else, but even as useful as it is for sustaining it, the Sovereigns aren't quite wholly sure to what end it plays. It works, and it works well, and that is enough for now. They will Duel with Fate should the time come that the Score leads them to ruin. They might very well end up doing just that.<br /> <br /> In the meantime, the Sovereigns run the city, using the Duels to sustain the Bulwark, resolve conflicts between parties, entertain the masses, and unite the Parisian Lost by Courting them one to another. The citizenry know that they push this system, but they live their lives unaware of just how much the Sovereigns of Paris rule all the aspects of their life.<br /> <br /> <b><u>NEXT TIME: The Duels and the Courted</u></b><br /> <br /> *I'm thinking of allowing people with skills of three or higher also attempt it with a -2 penalty.<br /> <br /> **Okay, yes, I'm a playtester. No, I'm not explaining that. Yes, I will edify once David puts up a blog on it. Wait your turn.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 07-01-2015, 10:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-01T12:10:16-07:00","id":575463,"title":"","content":"Council for the good of the Freehold or not, authority figures with very powerful divination tool make a nice recipe for suspicion!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-01T15:04:35-07:00","id":575707,"title":"","content":"The Score's going to be a thing I have to fine tune(heh) and figure out how powerful a divination tool I want it to be. I also have some thoughts on expanding it's capabilities in line with what they're doing with it.<br /> <br /> But yeah, they mean well, but the bottom line is simple: If you're a Sovereign, you're a manipulator, and probably an asshole, and you will make things worse for some people. There's a reason they keep this a secret from the people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-01T16:41:30-07:00","id":575768,"title":"","content":"Aaaaand Updated the Sovereigns. In there, I have refined the Score and revealed the dirty secret of the Sovereigns...as well as my own.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-01T22:13:49-07:00","id":576033,"title":"","content":"<u><b>SIDEBAR: The Reach of the Score and the Grasp of the Sovereigns</b></u><br /> <br /> How far can the Score go? Pretty far. The Score is broken up into twelve sections based on the times of the Zodiac for the span of a year, and the Sovereigns can use the Score to divine and render their powers as the Score permits in terms of space. Of course, the Bulwark's resetting gets in the way quite often, but asides from that, the Score gives the Sovereigns a lot of latitude. It's spatial limits extend only as far as the freehold's territory in the Hedge goes and to the edge of Paris Metropolitan Area, but the temporal reach is neat.<br /> <br /> Still, reaching that far and keeping grip on the events is a hard affair to do. Changes in the present can completely change the context that caused a Sovereign to make the choices they did. For this reason, most Sovereigns keep their plans within the next month.<br /> <br /> <b><u>SIDEBAR: The Lost Serpent</u></b><br /> <br /> What of Ophiucus, the thirteenth Zodiac? Good question. It's not present on the Sphere Score, but there are times where specialists feel like the conclusions they reach should land on a star that isn't there, and such calculations would work with the Serpent Constellation as a factor. Quite a few of the astrologers employed by the Sovereigns feel like the Score is missing, and that this is what partially causes some of the inexplicable failures of the Sphere Score. The Sovereigns don't have answers, or if they do, they're keeping tight lipped about it. Wayward who learn of the Sphere Score often look sideways at the Water Between, and wonder if Marcel took something with him to the grave.<br /> <br /> <u><b>The Duels and the Courted</b></u><br /> <br /> Originally, the dueling system employed in the early days of the freehold were intended as a means of giving war-torn changelings an outlet for their addictions to the fighting, a way to sort of deal with the trauma head on, and a means of restoring grandeur to the identity of the Parisian Lost. Marcel Bellerose based the idea off of the <i>narrative</i> of the Court of the Sun King as much as the reality, and in fact he fabricated the idea that Louis the XIV approved of them himself(in truth, he outright banned the practice in his time-not that stopped the aristocracy or the Lost). In short, it was a balm on freehold ripped by World War II, and it was treated as such by being turned into something of a spectacle. Duels were to be publicly announced and arranged, and while resources were still being gathered in the early days, effort was made to make them as big and grand as possible within the limits of venue and finance. Participants were made into legends, conflicts were blown into grand and twisting narratives of rivalry and disgust, people were invited, food was served, etc. It was a violent affair, but it was also a well-played game to build community and spirit. And if it was bound to the Wyrd and the Bulwark, it was only in the spirit of both stories inviting improvement from conflict, and even then only in a loose sense.<br /> <br /> A lot has changed since then. The first, and most important, came when the Sovereigns solidly connected the Duels to the Bulwark as a way to refine participants into potential Roles. The decision to encourage courtiers to use the Duels to demand that others prove their exemplary nature tied into some of the other factors of the time, such as the population becoming less war-torn and more concerned with lawful justice. As a result, the Duels diversified and became less violent, thought sword and pistol retained their popularity as cinema and literature began exploring the romance of the swashbuckler. Still, while more people took the option of keeping their duels private, the spectacle of the event has become one of the hardest things to shake from the ritual. Whether a bartender's duel, a Hedge race, or a trial-by-combat, courtiers are still encouraged, by court and by populace, to make a duel at least publicly known for the benefit of community. In fact, several courtiers make a career out of dueling, earning popularity and money by putting on a weekly show-in fact, one changeling has set up a Patreon-like website dedicated to supporting &quot;artists of conflict&quot;.<br /> <br /> Still, spectacle plays second to the use of the Duels as conflict resolution. Paris has law enforcement and due process, but the majority of the freehold's laws push for a duel to resolve most civil affairs. Grevious affairs such as conspiracy, rape, large-scale extortion schemes and things of the like are weighed on by the Sovereigns and their enforcement with grim severity, and all else is a matter for Dueling. Murder someone in secret and you'll be hunted down and made to pay dearly. Challenge someone to a sniper duel and win and the freehold officials will normally just grudgingly nod its' head. Fortunately, thanks to the rules of the duel and the Wyrd, duels to the death aren't actually that common. Most of the time, the outcome doesn't hurt anyone more than loss of pride and resources.<br /> <br /> The rules of the duel are fairly simple. The first is that any Duel must be agreed upon by way of [pledges, it's a thing with pledges]. If a [pledge] is removed at any point, the Duel is considered off. The second rule is that the Duel must reflect on the nature of the grievance and conflict. If the matter is one freeholder making another overpay for their apartment, expect the duel to involve money, housing, judgment, or other such things in the duel. The third rule is that a Freehold official must at least know of the duel, although several changelings ignore this one, and while the law enforcement of the freehold gets riled up, the Sovereigns never rush responses to such infractions. Nevertheless, an objective eye is expected to be kept to maintain a sense of fairness that the Wyrd sometimes overlooks. The fourth rule is that once the terms are agree upon, the duelists are not to interfere with the arrangement of a duel personally. And the fifth rule is that every duelist must have a second to prepare the field of conflict and to stand in the fight if needed, called the Courted(more on them in a bit).<br /> <br /> The rules also state the rights for challenger and defender. The Challenger, usually being the one with the grievance, gets to decide the form of the duel, within the agreement of the Wyrd on the second rule, and may also set the level of stakes for the conflict, though it must be equal on both sides in terms of the scale of loss. The Defender gets to decide on the location and the time of the duel. They must agree on an official to inform (if they bother with that), and agree upon the wording of the [pledge], with each side contributing (a common reason the Lost agree to the officials rule).<br /> <br /> In the early days, the seconds of duelists were expected to swear to the same terms(although admittedly, the same rules were not in place at the time), but things changed with failure of the Bulwark in 1968, running concurrently with the student riots. Faced again with the threat of True Fae and Huntsmen, a sardonic joke emerged from the community of courtiers no longer duelling, saying that if they gained anything from the freehold, it was a magnificent buddy system, as most courtiers turned to their seconds to watch their backs. Once re-established, the Sovereigns picked up the joke and turned into a new facet of the Duels. Their motives were to strengthen the community and ties as well as to create another way in which to filled the roles. With these goals in mind, they elevated the jokes of buddies and the old realities of seconds into the Courted.<br /> <br /> Strictly speaking, the Courted's role is not wholly different from seconds before hand, in the sense that they support the Duelists and are willing to stand in for them should cirucmstance demand it. But with their introduction, responsibilities of arranging the field of honor in preparation fell to them, as was, in general, just preparing the duelist in any way possible. Whether it was hiding weaponry around the field, stocking the bars, or making love to the duelist to ease their nerves, a Courted is expected to work hard enough in the favor of their duelist in preparation for the duel so as to create and strengthen the bond between them.<br /> <br /> The Courted, at the beginning of the duel, are required to do at least one act of preparation that involves a dice role, whether it an Intelligence+Brawl to give them point, Manipulation+Expression to psych them up, or any other combination that makes sense. The amount of preparation they performed before the duel gives them a bonus or penalty to this roll, up to +5 or -5. For each success the Courted gains, the duelist may use their highest Power, Finesse, and/or Resistance Attributes in place of the ones they'd normally use for the Duel. For example, if a Courted earns two successes with this roll, the Duelist could choose to use her highest Power and Finesse Attributes, or her highest Resistance and Power. The player can also stunt as though in a dream if the Courted is successful. Exceptional successes also allow the duelist to use their Wyrd as an equipment bonus to everything. Dramtatic Failure forces the duelist to use their lowest Attributes in all categories.<br /> <br /> A duel area that has one or more successful Courted attempts is still mired in reality, and yet takes on a dream like-quality, as do the duelists who benefited from their Courted. Everything is heightened, lays of physics seem to suspend just a little, and duelists seem to find just the right thing unexpectedly or pull out the right sort of twist. Duels like this always seem to improve someone in terms of their qualities for a Role, and it's no wonder the Final Duel always has this sort of effect.<br /> <br /> <b><u>SIDEBAR: What About Dream Duels?</u></b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Oneiromancy is a popular way to have duels-spectacle, privacy, and meaning can all be derived easily from it. So how do the Courted bless these duels?<br /> <br /> Their efforts are up to them to figure out, but the success they roll right before the Duel are added as a permanent dice pool modifier to all rolls. The Duelist just haw more potential to work with.<br /> <br /> A special exception exists if the Courted offer up their dreams for the stage. It's rare for this to happen, given the advantage, but it does on occasion work out this way. In these cases, a courted who succeeds on their roll makkes it so Environmental Actions made are always a teamwork roll, the Courted rolling the dice pool before the Changeling acts.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> One of the unspoken benefits to the Courted is the fact that they aren't quite bound by the [pledge] as the duelist. Courted tend to fight each other in various ways in the process of setting up and even during the duel as a way of psychological warfare. While torturing, brutally wounding, killing, or threatening any of these things or other similar actions in the middle of the duel is a quick way to earn a trip to the gallows of the guillotine, it can be heartening to look over and see your Courted wrestling the enemies ally to the ground successfully. And in the middle of this duel to prepare Duelists, Courted often display qualities of their own that incline them toward the Roles, giving the Sovereigns more people to pull into play should they need to.<br /> <br /> Motleys tend to emerge from duelists developing Courted for various kinds of events, and the freeholders of Paris are quite sure that few other cities have as many tightly knit groups. Still, not every day is a duel, and there's life beyond the Bulwark and it's maintenance.<br /> <br /> <b><u>NEXT TIME, MAYBE: What To Do Beyond Duels and the Bulwark.</u></b><br /> <br /> Hmm. I feel something is lacking.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-03T13:14:58-07:00","id":577649,"title":"","content":"<b><u>SIDEBAR: 1001 Problems but a Belle Ain't One of Them</u></b><br /> <br /> The city of Paris has a significant population of immigrants, particularly Muslims, and to say this has caused conflict in the past is a significant understatement. Paris is quite a secular city, and while the city is more open and welcoming towards American and other European travelers than expected, there's a troubled history with how they have handled people from African and Middle Eastern nations. This has built up on top of the city's significant history of ill-treatment of Eastern European and Roma people in the past, and Paris is home from everything to ill-maintained neighborhoods around Islamic mosques to outright slums housing the dislocated and lost.<br /> <br /> The Parisian freehold has less issues with race, national identity, and religion than most of their peers-Changelings have experienced a wider variety of things thanks to their Durances and are more open minded as a result. The freehold has been willing to graft in ideas and people to the positions and functions it fosters, and as newer minds come in, the boundaries that exist continue to break down. Where they aren't willing to compromise, however, is the Bulwark itself, and this is where changeling and mortal politics parallel.<br /> <br /> Immigrant changelings have always brought their freeholds with them and continued their practices as best as they can. This has always led to conflict with the Rose Courts, who have argued everything from these systems disrupting the ritual of the Bulwark to creating a deadzone that their Bulwark can't touch. The latest and most troublesome version has been the Scheherazade Freehold put in place, folding in the earlier Roma and Slavic practices into their court systems. Each side points at the failings of the other, the Scheherazade Courts point at the long gap of time the city is exposed if their Bulwark breaks, and the Rose Courts pointing out how often the opportunity for breaking happens with their own.<br /> <br /> The Scheherazade Freehold is not big enough to confront the Rose Freehold, and the Sovereigns tend to push for a &quot;live and let live&quot; attitude, making conflict with the Scheherazade one of the few non-grievous acts that receives a harsh penalty. Still, it is a significant enough thorn in the side of changelings that it becomes the vehicle for other issues that arise with the immigrant population, and small skirmishes happen yearly. Still, rumors in the last ten years have emerged of the Scheherazade Courts making allies with cults of the ancients ones that put the Rose Courts on edge, and there's some question of when and how the conflict of Bulwark will be dealt with in the coming years.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 07-03-2015, 05:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-03T14:02:03-07:00","id":577747,"title":"","content":"Nice nod to courts other than the Rose Courts and what their presence and ideas mean for the politics of the Freehold.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-03T14:45:15-07:00","id":577795,"title":"","content":"I intended to bring up that point with the Sovereigns, but I forgot. By and large I wanted to explore the idea of Paris's problems with the Arabic and other such community in more heavily Promethean and Mummy, but considering I quite like the Scheherazade Freehold, I figured this was a good way to tie it in. That and it's never a subject I want to ignore-Paris has had some really big riots over the subject in the past few years, and while I knew the reasons couldn't be the same, I wanted to ensure each game I potentially run in anticipation of the crossover game at least gives a nod to it. Plus I like the idea of an outside commentator on the somewhat rickety nature of the Rose Freehold in the vein of &quot;The story is as much about the reading as it is the text&quot;.<br /> <br /> But yeah, some of the stuff I've found in regards to racism and issues of religion in Paris has been chilling, and I really didn't want to ignore it even if I figured it wasn't going to be a major facet of the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-03T15:05:54-07:00","id":577813,"title":"","content":"It can prove to be a pretty ugly subject, yeah. All the more reason why it shouldn't be ignored, as you said.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T23:11:07-07:00","id":635457,"title":"","content":"So, I wrote up a tentative Mantle setup for the Rose Courts(<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=559072#post559072\">Here if you forgot them!</a>). I'm not a hundred percent sold on it. Also, I haven't figured out the Goblin Vows on each of them.<br /> <br /> Also, obviously, I can't explain everything in this. If you aren't sure about something, ask me, and if I can chat about it, I will.<br /> <br /> THAT SAID!<br /> <br /> <u><b>White Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: +1 Empathy<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: Rigid Mask<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: +1 Willpower<br /> <br /> <u><b>Red Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: +1 Persuasion<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: Sympathetic<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: True Friend<br /> <br /> <u><b>Blue Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: Acute Senses<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: +1 Initative<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: Re-roll failures on Clarity Breaking Points<br /> <br /> <u><b>Black Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: +1 to Intimidate<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: Danger Sense<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: +1 Health<br /> <br /> <u><b>Gold Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: +1 Socialize<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: Eidetic Memory<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: Arcadian Metabolism","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 08-22-2015, 02:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-26T21:58:58-07:00","id":641104,"title":"","content":"What can I say about this? My hope is that these are ST options included alongside rich details on the Seasons and other (perhaps more unique?) court structures. As the risk of sounding lazy, I'm already at the end of my patience with tool-kit settings. As an ST I feel obligated to fill in plenty of details in WOD settings, way more than I ever did in classic WOD settings. Freedom to color outside of the lines is great, being handed a box of crayons and told to draw the next Mona Lisa....not so much. I like the option to default where and when I want so that the process is a little less exhausting. And as a player, I like the chance to delve into a book to give my basic concepts more depth. I'm not really looking forward to having to hold off coming up with my character until I get a rundown of the local setting factions from my ST. Worse, 90% of fiction is crap. Which means 90% of ST concepts is crap. I'm already wary of the day I'll be earning to play an Autumn Court magister while I'm stuck playing the jester of the Gumdrop Loli court or whatever. In short, I'm concerned that this edition is placing TOO MUCH responsibility on the ST being creative. There's an important distinction to be found between giving the Storyteller creative options and forcing creative obligations on him.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-26T22:05:24-07:00","id":641108,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothri</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=641104#post641104\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What can I say about this? My hope is that these are ST options included alongside rich details on the Seasons and other (perhaps more unique?) court structures. As the risk of sounding lazy, I'm already at the end of my patience with tool-kit settings. As an ST I feel obligated to fill in plenty of details in WOD settings, way more than I ever did in classic WOD settings. Freedom to color outside of the lines is great, being handed a box of crayons and told to draw the next Mona Lisa....not so much. I like the option to default where and when I want so that the process is a little less exhausting. And as a player, I like the chance to delve into a book to give my basic concepts more depth. I'm not really looking forward to having to hold off coming up with my character until I get a rundown of the local setting factions from my ST. Worse, 90% of fiction is crap. Which means 90% of ST concepts is crap. I'm already wary of the day I'll be earning to play an Autumn Court magister while I'm stuck playing the jester of the Gumdrop Loli court or whatever. In short, I'm concerned that this edition is placing TOO MUCH responsibility on the ST being creative. There's an important distinction to be found between giving the Storyteller creative options and forcing creative obligations on him.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or you can just choose from one of the five pre-built settings, if that's what you want. <br /> <br /> Also, the Storyteller isn't the only person expected to participate in Court creation. If you're playing with a particularly uncreative table, you should probably use a pre-generated court. I personally avoid playing with uncreative people whenever possible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>David A Hill Jr </b><br /> Freelance Writer<br /> Independent Game Designer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/70-machineiv","name":"MachineIV","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar70_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1791"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-26T22:06:42-07:00","id":641109,"title":"","content":"The Seasonal Courts are still covered, and it's not like you can't use the old books.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-27T02:23:15-07:00","id":641293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=635457#post635457\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, I wrote up a tentative Mantle setup for the Rose Courts(<a href=\"http://&quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=559072#post559072&quot;]Here</a> if you forgot them![/URL]). I'm not a hundred percent sold on it. Also, I haven't figured out the Goblin Vows on each of them.<br /> <br /> Also, obviously, I can't explain everything in this. If you aren't sure about something, ask me, and if I can chat about it, I will.<br /> <br /> THAT SAID!<br /> <br /> <u><b>White Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: +1 Empathy<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: Rigid Mask<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: +1 Willpower<br /> <br /> <u><b>Red Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: +1 Persuasion<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: Sympathetic<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: True Friend<br /> <br /> <u><b>Blue Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: Acute Senses<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: +1 Initative<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: Re-roll failures on Clarity Breaking Points<br /> <br /> <u><b>Black Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: +1 to Intimidate<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: Danger Sense<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: +1 Health<br /> <br /> <u><b>Gold Rose</b></u><br /> 1-Dot: +1 Socialize<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow<br /> 3-Dot: Eidetic Memory<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: Arcadian Metabolism</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't want to sound tiresome, but since goblin vows seems to be more relevant in this edition, is there someone in the Team who can talk us a bit about them, and how they tie to the Courts ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2411-raistlin","name":"Raistlin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"713"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-27T02:39:43-07:00","id":641298,"title":"","content":"Not quite yet. It's being worked on.<br /> <br /> Believe me, I asked about this not long ago.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-27T03:47:57-07:00","id":641345,"title":"","content":"Hey ArcaneArts, curious question. In your examples on the courts you name the Maiden, The woodsman and The Wild One. Did I miss something somewhere, or did you just make them up as archetypes to understand the courts better?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6030-a_newfie","name":"A_Newfie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-27T17:59:35-07:00","id":642272,"title":"","content":"I needed words to contextualize the idea of the ritual based on Riding Hood and Beauty and the Beast, words the represented strong bases for a variety of ideas that could also capture Utena's ideas that are found within the Prince, the Princess, and the Witch. Originally, it was Maiden, Beast, and Huntsman. I traded out beast for Wild One because I felt like using Beast as a ritual title in a world where people identifies as beasts (and Beasts, and then Beasts) was awkward, and Woodsman instead of Huntsman because I traded part of the original conceit(part of the reason the Bulwark works is because the Courts hunt their own and therefore didn't need Huntsmen) off to the piecemeal freehold during the War.<br /> <br /> On a simple basis, it lines up neatly-Maidens are for Little Red and Belle, Wild Ones are for the Wolf and Beast, and Woodsman are for Avenant(Gaston) and the Lumberjack. If I could find better words that fit the very broad symbol base I have in mind for designing the freehold, I'd run with that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 08-27-2015, 11:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-27T20:17:17-07:00","id":642414,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MachineIV</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=641108#post641108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Or you can just choose from one of the five pre-built settings, if that's what you want. <br /> <br /> Also, the Storyteller isn't the only person expected to participate in Court creation. If you're playing with a particularly uncreative table, you should probably use a pre-generated court. I personally avoid playing with uncreative people whenever possible.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'm personally going with Toronto, the level of complexity and politics of the court system for the Toronto Freehold is huge, you have 4 complex courts with internal factions, 1 rogue lost court plotting its rebirth, 2 local rebellion freeholds that oppose the main Toronto Freehold and each use a completely different court system, a human mystery cult tied to the Ivory Court, and the Hobgoblin politics of the huge Goblin Market in Toronto<br /> <br /> On top of that is the usual threats and plots of Loyalists and Huntsmen, and the mortal we world.<br /> <br /> I'm creative enough to come up with my own setting (I might as well, not to play but for creative expression), but I'd rather apply my imagination towards further fleshing out the Toronto setting with details about Toronto and my own creative twists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-27T20:34:40-07:00","id":642478,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=642414#post642414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm personally going with Toronto, the level of complexity and politics of the court system for the Toronto Freehold is huge, you have 4 complex courts with internal factions, 1 rogue lost court plotting its rebirth, 2 local rebellion freeholds that oppose the main Toronto Freehold and each use a completely different court system, a human mystery cult tied to the Ivory Court, and the Hobgoblin politics of the huge Goblin Market in Toronto<br /> <br /> On top of that is the usual threats and plots of Loyalists and Huntsmen, and the mortal we world.<br /> <br /> I'm creative enough to come up with my own setting (I might as well, not to play but for creative expression), but I'd rather apply my imagination towards further fleshing out the Toronto setting with details about Toronto and my own creative twists.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. <br /> <br /> I'm going to be running a game soon, and I'm debating running Toronto instead of Tokyo, which I would be doing to flesh out my own work a little. I really enjoyed the Toronto setup.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>David A Hill Jr </b><br /> Freelance Writer<br /> Independent Game Designer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/70-machineiv","name":"MachineIV","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar70_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1791"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-27T20:44:21-07:00","id":642510,"title":"","content":"The Niagra Falls Freehold.<br /> <br /> The America Court and the Canada Court.<br /> <br /> The America Courts emotion Patriotism, Canada Court emotion is Doubt. <br /> <br /> Symbols of the America Court, the Bald Eagle, the American part of Niagra Falls, symbols of America, and Symbols of Faith.<br /> <br /> The Canada Court symbols are The Beaver, the Canadian section of Niagra Falls, Symbols of Canada, and Symbols of Skepticism.<br /> <br /> The Seasonal Court in Fort Errie has given the Niagra Falls Freehold alot of problems lately, which has driven it to make an alliance between the Niagra Falls Freehold and the Toronto Freehold to deal with Seasonal Court (Seelie Court) rebellions and the threat posed by they're international allies.<br /> <br /> Another Court idea, the Freehold of Montreal, aka the Erotic Dungeon Freehold.<br /> <br /> The Court of Dominance, The Court of Submission, and The Court of the Switch.<br /> <br /> Court of Dominance emotion, Pain, symbols the whip, the riding crop, the scream, and leather.<br /> <br /> The Court of the Submissive, aka the Slave's Court, emotion pleasure, the hand cuffs, ball gags, rope, bondage tape, silk, chokers, and orgasms.<br /> <br /> The Court of the Switch, Court emotion is anticaption, feathers, blind folds, more intimate sex toys, laughter.<br /> <br /> Friendly Rivaltry/Alliance with the Toronto Freehold and Niagra Falls Freehold, at war with the Seelie Courts of Quebec City.<br /> <br /> Just to show I can come up with my own ideas for interesting courts although I have no plans for actually using them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2241,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","user":"Omegaphallic","date":". 08-27-2015, 11:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-27T21:09:04-07:00","id":642525,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MachineIV</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=642478#post642478\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Agreed. <br /> <br /> I'm going to be running a game soon, and I'm debating running Toronto instead of Tokyo, which I would be doing to flesh out my own work a little. I really enjoyed the Toronto setup.</div> </div> </div> </div> Actually one cool thing you could do is a dual cities setting.<br /> <br /> I'm toying with the idea that Toronto and Miami would go well together, Toronto and Miami have more combined hooks then you'd think, more connections.<br /> <br /> I'd call it the Snowbird Chronicles, and build a kind of cold war plotline, with plots inside plots, the Miami Court uses the Seasonal/Seelie Rebel Freeholds, and sympathisers in the Silver Court, to spy and undermine the Toronto Freehold and the Toronto Freehold using snowbirds in the Cult of Mithra, and antisummer forces in the Miami Freehold to spy and undermine them in turn. The unofficial Ruby Court would be trying to turn up the heat, manipulating both sides to increase the conflict and the Ruby Court would be secretly being influenced by Loyalists, unknown to the regular serious members trying to rebuild the Ruby Court, the Loyalists in turn would be manipulated in turn by group of Rogue Huntsmen, who serve an unknown none True Fae Enitity with its own agenda.<br /> <br /> Just some starting ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2241,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","user":"Omegaphallic","date":". 08-28-2015, 12:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T05:03:09-07:00","id":642845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MachineIV</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=641108#post641108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, the Storyteller isn't the only person expected to participate in Court creation. If you're playing with a particularly uncreative table, you should probably use a pre-generated court. I personally avoid playing with uncreative people whenever possible.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh? Beyond their own characters (and ambitions for the courts) I was kind of imagining player-participation in creation might strain the delicate balance of groups and mythos. I don't think Arcane could have made the Rose Court so deep by committee. :-)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3070-barbrarilen","name":"Barbrarilen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3070_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"268"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T09:23:42-07:00","id":643057,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Barbrarilen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=642845#post642845\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh? Beyond their own characters (and ambitions for the courts) I was kind of imagining player-participation in creation might strain the delicate balance of groups and mythos. I don't think Arcane could have made the Rose Court so deep by committee. :-)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I think the idea is that everyone at the table has some creative input, but then the ST fleshes things out. ArcaneArts' Freehold is pretty conceptually unified, much like a Seasonal Courts Freehold would be. That's still possible with a group having creative input, but you have to have a group that's willing to cooperate and agree on a solid theme. Toronto's Courts are far more disparate, really only being joined by the stroytelling ritual that makes up the Freehold's Bulwark. I would think that creating a Freehold with vastly disparate Courts brought together under a broadly applicable and inclusive Bulwark would be even easier to do as a group endeavor. Both styles have their advantages. With a more cohesive theme for a Freehold, you get more shared culture and traditions and a sense of unity even among the different Courts, which allows you to really focus on exploring their customs and ideals. With widely different groups united by a flexible Bulwark, you get the drama of culture clash and, likely, a more polarized, divisive atmosphere, which is great for a politically-focused game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T09:27:19-07:00","id":643060,"title":"","content":"Side note to ArcaneArts:<br /> <br /> Have you considered giving the Blue Rose Court something like Patient or Good Time Management instead of Acute Senses for their Mantle 1 benefit? Fits better with the &quot;lessons of patience and haste&quot; bit, I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T10:45:37-07:00","id":643173,"title":"","content":"Patience and haste lead to Clarity. But it;s something to chew on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T10:52:42-07:00","id":643180,"title":"","content":"Hey Arc do you have a pdf file of all your Paris stuff?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCxrkv14ojD_TQXU0RBvF33Q\" target=\"_blank\">Fool's Corner Productions</a>: Where we review Video Game, and eventually tabletop RPGs<br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://dl.dropboxusercontent.com/u/7019410/Images/Ex3%20KS%20Badge.gif\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img31/2512/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/461-kingcarnival","name":"KingCarnival","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar461_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"170"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T11:00:19-07:00","id":643185,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=643173#post643173\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Patience and haste lead to Clarity. But it;s something to chew on.</div> </div> </div> </div>I get that, but you have a benefit dealing with both haste (+1 initiative) and Clarity (re-rolling breaking points), but nothing for patience. Seems like it would make sense for patience and haste to be represented by the earlier evolutions of the mantle, with the Clarity benefit as the culmination.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T11:07:20-07:00","id":643189,"title":"","content":"On Court Creation-The problem with the Rose Courts is that there's a lack of dimensionality to them. That comes from just one set of eyes looking over them and one mind working out the details. The additional perspectives allow for the courts to be far more dynamic and fleshed out.<br /> <br /> As a rule, the Storyteller is an arbiter in all things. The group throws out things into the stew, and the storyteller picks out a thread from them and begins weaving a pattern out of them, with the advice of the group. It's a group activity, but the Storyteller has the final say on things.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T11:11:05-07:00","id":643191,"title":"","content":"The Blue Rose Mantle brings up a weird quirk of the Court Creation system; why, when designing your Court Mantle, would you opt for the +1 to speed or initiative when you could get a two dot merit instead and take Fleet of Foot or Fast Reflexes for +2? The only reasoning I can think of is so that you could stack the merits with the Mantle bonus, but that seems illogical (or, at least, excessively min-max-y) since it means making the Mantle weaker for most of the Court so that a few characters can squeeze an extra point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T11:21:19-07:00","id":643194,"title":"","content":"On the Quirk: Yeah, I saw that when I was looking over the Creation system just now. No good way to handle that in the short term.<br /> <br /> On the Blue Rose: Mantle: That's actually a really good point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T11:23:50-07:00","id":643197,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>KingCarnival</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=643180#post643180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey Arc do you have a pdf file of all your Paris stuff?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's still super messy. I want to go over a few more itmes, clean it, fill in some of the mechanics that are still absent, and get some more write up on the city. I want to make a proper PDF.<br /> <br /> And since I'm making a second city(thinking somewhere in Russia or India), that's gonna be a bit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-28T11:41:19-07:00","id":643207,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=643194#post643194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the Quirk: Yeah, I saw that when I was looking over the Creation system just now. No good way to handle that in the short term.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I'd just remove the +1 option for initiative and speed. Let people take the 2 dot merits if they want. I feel like you should get more out of Mantle 3 than the equivalent of a 1 dot merit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T22:55:09-07:00","id":711857,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=567151#post567151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(No, actually I'm working on making a few Freeholds for Colorado, and I'm going to have them all use stories that feature Big Bad Wolves.)</div> </div> </div> </div>So, for anyone who might be interested (I know Arcane said he couldn't wait), I am still working on this. It's gone through some changes and it's looking like it's actually just going to be one Freehold instead of multiple, but it also won't be as specifically tied to my Colorado setting, so it will be more easily usable in Werewolf/Changeling crossover games other than my own. I've had a sudden wave of inspiration and it's finally making significant progress, so I will post it as soon as I have a solid first draft.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T03:28:04-07:00","id":718451,"title":"","content":"Firstly I'd like to say thanks very much for this. I'm in the south west of Australia and always had issues around the seasonal courts since our 'seasons' are limited. I've been developing a new chronicle for a while now and toyed with a few options but eventually went with two courts based on night and day. One court uses the Light/Dark duality while the other side uses Day/Night titles. All to do with the perceptions held and agendas being pushed.<br /> <br /> I had a few things I was changing, selecting abilities for mantle ratings, contracts for each court and building bonuses based on the time of day all working out of 1st ed. I'm not going to shift to 2nd ed since there are a few core mechanics I don't like much but this Freehold doc will help a lot with fleshing out and organising some of the thoughts I had already. I really want to enjoy 2nd ed but just can't get over a few issues. many of my players feel the same way unfortunately. I'll keep looking at the new stuff coming and hopefully our troupe/groups will eventually consider making the shift. But right now, just don't think we can. Absolutely loving some of the background material and storyteller utility items coming out. And Dark Era and cross-genre stuff...just wow.<br /> <br /> wave<br /> ad","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8747-ad1","name":"Ad1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T07:33:19-07:00","id":719029,"title":"","content":"Um. Okay then.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-24T17:03:04-07:00","id":724509,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ad1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=718451#post718451\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Firstly I'd like to say thanks very much for this. I'm in the south west of Australia and always had issues around the seasonal courts since our 'seasons' are limited. I've been developing a new chronicle for a while now and toyed with a few options but eventually went with two courts based on night and day. One court uses the Light/Dark duality while the other side uses Day/Night titles. All to do with the perceptions held and agendas being pushed.<br /> <br /> I had a few things I was changing, selecting abilities for mantle ratings, contracts for each court and building bonuses based on the time of day all working out of 1st ed. I'm not going to shift to 2nd ed since there are a few core mechanics I don't like much but this Freehold doc will help a lot with fleshing out and organising some of the thoughts I had already. I really want to enjoy 2nd ed but just can't get over a few issues. many of my players feel the same way unfortunately. I'll keep looking at the new stuff coming and hopefully our troupe/groups will eventually consider making the shift. But right now, just don't think we can. Absolutely loving some of the background material and storyteller utility items coming out. And Dark Era and cross-genre stuff...just wow.<br /> <br /> wave<br /> ad</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't intend on changing anything I haven't explicitly mentioned yet, but I'm curious, what parts can't you get over? I've found that for some people, these issues aren't actually issues; it's just a little clarity and presentation problem.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>David A Hill Jr </b><br /> Freelance Writer<br /> Independent Game Designer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/70-machineiv","name":"MachineIV","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar70_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1791"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-06T11:55:15-08:00","id":759108,"title":"","content":"So I am working on a Portland Oregon setting, and I am thinking of actually setting the protagonists in Vancouver Washington and having the Portland Freehold being Privateers with a shanghai 1 hapless mortal for every 10 Lost belonging to the freehold, and a single outsider changeling for a group sacrifice for the freehold every year for their Bulwark. A Portland Privateers freehold if you will, filled with selfish, ruthless and pretentious Lost that act as a foil for the PC lost in Vancouver. The Vancouver changelings will hate the Portland changelings because those people are the ones that stole their lives. Possibly the monarchs of Portland get Fetch servants out of the deal. See someone you like, and you're a Portland Freehold member? Catch that person and trade them in to get a fetch version of them that obeys you.<br /> <br /> Also one of the real life problems of Portland Oregon is human trafficking so this plays into that theme.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-05T18:54:47-08:00","id":780848,"title":"","content":"Hope I'm not necro'ing or anything. I found this system yesterday and I decided to do a proper Court system for the fictional town where I'm running my current game.<br /> <br /> - <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><u><b>The Thornwall Freehold</b></u></span></span></div> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><u><b>The Story</b></u></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><i>This is the tale of a woman. Fleeing persecution for her beliefs, she fled her home. She ran and ran, until one day she found a sacred place. A place of power and wonder. She claimed it as her own, building walls, weaving powerful enchantments and gathering allies. When her pursuers caught up to her, she fought them. She was felled in battle, but her sacrifice won the fight for her people.<br /> <br /> This is our tale, too. We fled Arcadia. We found this place. We made it stronger. We've woven our spells and built our walls. We've made allies out of mortals, wolves and the undead. Then, every year, we fight. Every year, we sacrifice. And every year, we keep this town safe.</i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Tale of the Good Witch is as old as the town itself. Indeed, that's more or less how the town came to be. The tale takes different forms, depending on who tells it and the message they attempt to convey. Sometimes, the protagonist isn't even a woman at all. The tale, however, must retain five key parts.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Exile: The tale must always begin with the protagonist fleeing from some great danger she's not powerful enough to face. Often, the protagonist is fleeing from her former home.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Discovery: The protagonist always discovers a source of strength. The source varies, but it usually tends to be a place, often with vaguely religious connotations.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Defence: The protagonist always seeks to protect this source of strength, with long-term defences that will stand the test of time. This is important, for it foreshadows the protagonist's foresight.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Alliance: The protagonist always finds allies, who are invariably very different from her. Emphasis is placed in how they do things she cannot do and vice versa.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Sacrifice: The protagonist always fights her pursuers in the end and always falls. In some stories, she doesn't die, her defeat is instead delivered in a different way. Regardless, the point of this part of the tale is to highlight the protagonist's foresight, foreshadowed in The Defence. The protagonist knows that her personal defeat will secure victory for her alliance, her source of power, and the legacy she's left behind (in the original tale, the legacy was the woman's husband and children).<br /> <br /> The story has many different versions, and changelings are fond of taking the frame and changing the details as much as possible while still preserving the core five parts.</span><br /> <br /> <u><b>The Bulwark</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The ritual to keep the True Fae at bay and establish the Bulwark is surprisingly simple. Every New Year's Eve, when everyone is waiting for the turn of the year, the entire Freehold enters the Hedge. Those who are too weak to fight, or cannot do so, stay by the Monarchs' side. Armed with blade and spell, the changelings challenge the Huntsmen and the Gentry, and fight any that show up. This battle is not undertaken lightly. An entire year's preparation go into it, with powerful enchantments, contracts and pledges carefully arrayed to counter any Gentry trickery.<br /> <br /> For the Bulwark to be effective, the challenge must be issued before midnight, and the battle must last at least until twelve minutes and twelve seconds have passed after midnight. If no Huntsmen or Gentry accept the Freehold's challenge, the Bulwark is completed successfully. Therefore, most years, the battle is quite dangerous as the True Fae give it their all. Changelings tell that at first, the battles were gruesome and bloody, with many of their number falling before the Freehold could retire back to the mortal world safely, the Bulwark completed. However, as time passed, the Courts refined their craft and perfected their mission. They don't seek to kill or even wound the Gentry, they just seek to fight until the allotted time. To minimise casualties, the Freehold prepares for most of the year. Combat drills, magical protections, artefacts of power and pledges of luck and fate, all intended to keep the changeling from being killed or dragged back to Arcadia.<br /> <br /> It is not uncommon for non-changelings to join in the fight, but it is never asked or even suggested. Some vampires or werewolves fight to get a taste of a Fae's blood or flesh, or a hunter may see fighting the Gentry as the purest form of the Vigil. Those that express their desire to fight are asked to sign up early in the year so that they can attend training and can be granted special protections.<br /> <br /> Regardless of how the fight goes, once the required time has passed, the Freehold must make a sacrifice. Originally, one of the changelings emulated the protagonist of the tale and remained behind in the Hedge, fighting to the death (though some were, rather unfortunately, recaptured and dragged back to Arcadia). As the Courts perfected their craft, they also found a way to avoid having to sacrifice one of their own. Throughout the year, the Freehold works on a project, usually a spell or a powerful artefact. During their yearly battle, just before they sound the retreat, they sacrifice their prized creation, destroying it in a way that harms the Huntsmen and Gentry they're battling. If this cannot be done, one of the changelings must sacrifice herself or be sacrificed by the Freehold. Should everything go well, then comes a well-practised withdrawal, traps for the pursuing Huntsmen are sprung, the gates are sealed, the walls are raised... and the Bulwark stands for another year.</span><br /> <br /> <u><b>The Courts</b></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Thornwall Freehold's courts are partly based on the tale and partly based on the Bulwark. All four Monarchs rule together as a council. Whenever there is a tie in votes, the most respected Vizier breaks the vote. When no Vizier is available, the most esteemed member of the Freehold (that is seen as a neutral third party) is summoned instead.</span><br /> <br /> <b><i>The Shield Court. The Gray Bastion. The Court of Walls and Gates.</i></b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;The walls will hold. Even if it kills me, the walls will hold.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Shield Court is the court of defence. It focuses on maintaining the powerful enchantments woven around town, policing the Hedge and casting defensive enchantments upon the warriors before battle. In battle, they use their tough builds, masterfully crafted armour or magical power to protect the main bulk of the Freehold from the Huntsmen's assault. In times of peace, the Shield Court focuses on maintaining vaults and safehouses, selling their services for favours or Pledges, and spending time with mortals.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Changelings that swear allegiance to the Gray Bastion tend to be guarded folk. They are so concerned (some would say obsessed) with safety that they miss out on valuable things. They're the ones that triple-check every lock of every door and window. The ones that refuse to take a chance if there's a significant chance of failure. The ones that keep their emotional walls up, even when it's slowly killing them.</span><br /> <br /> <i>Court Emotion: Safety.</i><br /> <i>Court Contracts: Contracts of Stone Walls. Contracts of Shielded Hearts.<br /> Court Heraldry: Gray, silver and blue as colours. Walls, shields, gates, towers and castles as symbols.</i><br /> <br /> <u>Approaches</u><br /> <br /> <i>Breach These Walls:</i><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> Whenever a Huntsman pierces the defences (whether physical, magical or emotional) of a Shield Courtier, he gains one Yearning.</span><br /> <i>The Shield Has Broken:</i><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> Whenever a Huntsman manages to harm one of the Shield Courtier's loved ones or allies, despite the Shield Courtier's best attempts to protect them, he gains one Yearning.</span><br /> <br /> <u>Court Mantle</u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Gray Bastion's mantle is not subtle. It is clear in its intent. The changeling looks armoured or protected in a way that reflects her talents. A magically-inclined changeling may have shields of force floating around her, while a martial changeling may wear metal or leather armour. Even a social changeling or a pledgemaster may be surrounded by protective voices or long strings of floating words.</span><br /> <br /> <b><i>The Alliance Court. The Verdant Promise. The Court of Pacts and Union.</i></b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;So, you finally tracked me down. Good job. See those yellow eyes in the dark over there? That's a werewolf. She's with me. Feel the cold in the air? You don't </i></span><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"><i>even </i></span><span style=\"font-family:times new roman\"><i>want to know what that is.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Alliance Court is the court that brings everyone together. It focuses on maintaining long-standing alliances between the Freehold and the other denizens of the World of Darkness, and bringing in new allies into the fold. In battle, they coordinate, lead and support, displaying a keen insight into the nature of synergy. A courtier of the Verdant Promise is well trained in seeing how different types of magic synergise and make each other stronger. In times of peace, the Alliance Court focuses on paying back their allies for their services, maintaining good relations with everyone and mediating in Freehold matters.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Changelings that swear allegiance to the Verdant Promise tend to be very good socialisers. Unfortunately, their tempered, moderate views can be off-putting to those that want someone to fully support them. An Alliance Courtier's insufferable penchant for &quot;seeing both sides of the issue&quot; and &quot;not taking sides&quot; and insisting on compromise and cooperation can really rub some people the wrong way. On the other hand, the Verdant Promise can also be seen as distant and manipulating, especially given their court emotion.</span><br /> <br /> <i>Court Emotion: Trust.<br /> Court Contracts: Contracts of Cooperation. Contracts of Allied Hearts.<br /> Court Heraldry: Green, gold and brown as colours. Clasping hands, knots, rings and other bindings as symbols.</i><br /> <br /> <u>Approaches</u><br /> <br /> <i>Broken Trust:</i><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> Whenever a Huntsman manages to arrange for an Alliance Courtier to betray (deliberately or accidentally) one of her allies, he gains one Yearning.</span><br /> <i>Sow Dissension:</i><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> Whenever a Huntsman manages to turn the allies of an Alliance Courtier against each other, despite the Alliance Courtier's opposition, he gains one Yearning.</span><br /> <br /> <u>Court Mantle</u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Verdant Promise's mantle is very subtle. It is the whispers of wise allies, the promise that she'll come for you if you need her, a flower given as a symbol of friendship, a pair of matched gold rings, These subtle things begin to appear around a changeling, tiny symbols of promise, trust, friendship and collaboration, slightly tailored towards whatever allies the changeling herself has made in favour of the Freehold.</span><br /> <br /> <b><i>The Martyr Court. The Scarlet Sacrifice. The Court of Blade and Blood.</i></b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;I don't think you understand. I don't care if you kill me. I'm going to make damn sure I make as many of you suffer and bleed until you finally take me out.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Martyr Court is the warrior court. Unlike the Gray Bastion's guarded emotions and the Verdant Promise's moderate stances, the Scarlet Sacrifice wears emotion on the sleeve. Martyr Courtiers are known for not holding back, for giving themselves over to whatever emotion they're feeling, and for putting all of themselves in everything they do. No other court matches the Scarlet Sacrifice's passion, nor its faith in the Freehold's ideals. In battle, the Martyr Court is at the front, fighting with all their might against the Huntsman and the Gentry, facing their fears head on and living for that rush of bravery. In times of peace, the Court of Blade and Blood trains warriors for battle, indulges in emotional pursuits (from art to duels to orgies) and seeks to live life to the fullest.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Changelings that swear allegiance to the Scarlet Sacrifice tend to be passionate and compelling, if not outright attractive. Of course, it's easy to consider them volatile, dangerous or even deranged. It's rare for changelings outside the Alliance Court to hold an even or moderate opinion on the Martyr Court. Some love them, some hate them, often both at once, very rarely neither.</span><br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Court Emotion: Courage.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Court Contracts: Contracts of Blood and Sacrifice, Contracts of Martyred Hearts.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\">Court Heraldry: Red, pink and orange as colours. Petals, blood, blades, smooth fabric and prostrate figures as symbols.</span></i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><u>Approaches</u><br /> <br /> <i>Blood Spilled In Vain:</i> When a Huntsman gets a Martyr Courtier to sacrifice something valuable (including herself) without a valid, important reason, he gains one Yearning.<br /> <i>Broken Martyr:</i> When a Huntsman ignores the sacrifice of a Martyr Courtier or spares her, when it would be convenient for him if he didn't, he gains one Yearning.<br /> <br /> <u>Court Mantle</u><br /> <br /> The Scarlet Sacrifice's mantle changes with the changeling's emotions, its effects unique to each specific changeling. It may be bloody when she's angry, dripping red rivulets down her face and arms, and then turn into a rich crimson silk wrapped around her like a shawl when she's feeling content. It can flare like heavy perfume when she's lustful or become a whirlwind of petals when she's happy. While some may think that such a mantle would be rather telling, the Martyr Courtiers rarely bother to conceal their emotions.<br /> <br /> <b><i>The Veil Court. The Violet Shroud. The Court of Secrets and Shadows.</i></b><br /> <br /> <i>&quot;You are never going to find out where I buried the amulet. Now get out of town, before I bury you, too.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> The Veil Court is the court of guardians and keepers of dangerous lore and artefacts. Over the many, many years since the town's foundation, the changelings of the Violet Shroud have buried secret after secret, concealed powerful relics and mystic incantations. Cautious by nature, the Veil Courtiers are loath to unearth anything they've consigned to the darkness, even if it might solve some predicament. For the Court of Secrets and Shadows, very little compares to the dangers of unchecked magic. In battle, the Veil Court protects the Monarchs and guards the creation that will be sacrificed when the time is right. They are also known for being quite sneaky and performing surprise attacks on Huntsmen and vulnerable Gentry. In times of peace, they strive to find dangerous magic to bury, they keep curious noses off anything important, and they sell their services of concealment to other members of the Freehold.<br /> <br /> Changelings that swear allegiance to the Violet Shroud tend to be secretive and stealthy. They tend to deal with threats in very final ways, and very rarely do they say everything they know about a subject. It's because of this that they can be seen as arrogant powermongers that seek only to inflate their own importance, suppress anything that threatens them and meddle in other changeling's affairs.<br /> <br /> <i>Court Emotion: Repression.<br /> Court Contracts: Contracts of Darkened Vaults. Contracts of Veiled Hearts.<br /> Court Heraldry: Black, purple and white as colours. Veils, earth, tombs, vaults, shrouds and shadows as symbols.</i><br /> <br /> <u>Approaches</u><br /> <br /> <i>A Secret Uncovered:</i> Whenever a Huntsman unearths something important that a Veil Courtier has concealed, he gains one Yearning.<br /> <i>To Lift The Veil:</i> Whenever a Huntsman reveals to mortals a dangerous magic or artefact that a Veil Courtier has hidden, he gains one Yearning.<br /> <br /> <u>Court Mantle</u><br /> <br /> The Violet Shroud's mantle is subtle and hard to spot. It often looks like some purple haziness around the changeling, or perhaps the thinnest and most diaphanous of fabrics hanging from her shoulders. Perhaps it makes the changeling always look slightly shadowed or blurry. In general, the mantle's nature and the specific part of the changeling's body that it attempts to conceal are both unique to each changeling.</span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":10056,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","user":"ShadowKnight1224","date":". 12-05-2015, 10:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-24T12:30:47-08:00","id":792302,"title":"","content":"Bumping for upcoming discussion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-01T21:29:54-08:00","id":798094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShadowKnight1224</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=780848#post780848\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hope I'm not necro'ing or anything. I found this system yesterday and I decided to do a proper Court system for the fictional town where I'm running my current game.</div> </div> </div> </div>This can definitely be a system that could be replicated for freeholds in other locations. Can we also expect Contract write-ups to pop up soon?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/806810-deionscribe-s-homebrew-hub#post806810\" target=\"_blank\">&quot;My Homebrew Hub&quot;</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/828132-open-invite-age-of-azar\" target=\"_blank\">Age of Azar</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/907224-the-kingdom-of-yamatai-dark-era\" target=\"_blank\">The Kingdom of Yamatai</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8180-deionscribe","name":"Deionscribe","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-07T11:28:49-08:00","id":803227,"title":"","content":"So, as it stands, I'm not very happy with the Bulwark I came up with for my Gold Mountain freehold. Annual Bulwark rituals are supposed to be uncommon and particularly onerous, but I've had trouble coming up with a thematically appropriate ritual that would take place more than once a year. I have had one idea, but it's a little odd, so I figured I'd throw it out there to see if it sounds appropriate to people:<br /> <br /> The Gold Mountain Bulwark is deceptively simple in principle, but more onerous than it may initially appear. All of the Gold Mountain changelings, from the time that they are formally sworn into the Freehold, must have at least one item made of gold on their person at all times. This can be a piece of jewelry, a piercing, or even something like a cuff link or a pin, though for obvious reasons no changeling would rely exclusively on an object which couldn't be worn while sleeping. Simply holding an item is enough, so a changeling with a gold ring could take it off and still meet the Bulwark's requirement as long as they didn't give it away or set it aside. Furthermore, qualifying items don't need to be pure gold (indeed, few are), but they must both contain some amount of actual gold and visibly display the distinctive color and luster of the element. The latter requirement means that alloys like white gold and rose gold <i>do not</i> satisfy the requirements of the Bulwark regardless of how much gold they actually contain, for the simple reason that they don't look right.<br /> <br /> In essence, the act of wearing gold is an extremely simple Bulwark ritual, but one which must be performed <i>constantly</i>, and in which the entire Freehold must participate. The Bulwark is very easy to break, even by accident, but it is usually also very easy to restore. However, should a Huntsman or other agent of the Others take even the most junior member of the Freehold hostage, they could easily suppress the Bulwark indefinitely until the prisoner was recovered, slain, or otherwise relieved of their oath to the Freehold. For this reason, the changelings of Gold Mountain have long maintained certain rituals and holidays under the pretense that they are essential to maintaining the Bulwark, in an effort to prevent the forces of Arcadia from discovering their vulnerability.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-07T12:01:31-08:00","id":803246,"title":"","content":"So, I've had some stupid, hair-brained ideas for Courts. I'd figure I'd throw my basic ideas out and see just how dumb they are.<br /> <br /> <b>Auspice Courts</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Unsure about location. Ruled by a council made up of each Court's &quot;Elder&quot;. Inspired by the Forsaken that the local Freehold have allied with for generations, the Story's themes and motifs are <i>Primal Savagery</i> and <i>Dark Animism</i>. Their Bulwark is a massive, many day Wild Hunt, where the Huntsmen seek out the Lost and the Uratha during the day, and they Freehold and their allies consecrate the earth with Huntsmen blood at night.<br /> \u2022New Moon(Patience)- Court of Stalkers-Those who wait. The Stalkers are creatures of stone and shadow, who wait like statues so they might descend swift as dusk. These Lost know the value of both perseverance and timing. <br /> \u2022Crescent Moon(Revelation)- Court of Shamans- Those who understand. The Shamans seek Revelations, the hard earned insight that comes like lightning, no matter if it is found in the burst of speed born of a runner's high or the rush of inspiration brought on by a looming deadline.<br /> \u2022Half-Moon(Serenity)-Court of Counselors. The oft-envied Counselors seek to advise and lead their fellow Lost as they proclaim the power of Serenity, the acceptance and balance of their twin natures. But it is so hard to practice what they preach, and many Counselors wear a pretty little mask even as they fight to preserve their souls... <br /> \u2022Gibbous Moon(Glory)-Court of Bards- Those who speak. Of all the Lost in the Auspice Freehold, it is the Bards who most embrace the fact that they live in stories. They are tale-tellers and song-singers, who remember and exult the fallen in all they do. But they are not just content with remembering; they seek to be remembered, preforming great deeds and glorious labors so that they might never be forgotten. <br /> \u2022Full Moon(Restraint)-Court of Fetters- Those who are bound. The Fettered live a life ruled by paradox. The Full Moon unleashes madness and wrath, but its Court denies instead of emulates it. This is the Court of slavery that is chosen, bindings that make one free, and obedience born of free will. To earn one's freedom, the Fettered whisper, one must throw it away. But only to one <b>you</b> chose.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <b>VIRTUE COURTS</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Unsure about location. Seven Courts who rule the Seven days of the week. The themes and motifs of their Story are <i>Virtue and Temptation</i>, <i>Angels and Abrahamic Iconography</i> and <i>Morality Plays</i>. The Bulwark is, in fact, a massive annual Morality Play, seven acts over seven nights, where the tempters are played by Huntsmen and where the temptations and consequences are oh so real. <br /> \u2022Charity-Sunday's Court, Michael's Court, the Violet Court, the Scale and the Sword, Court of Martyrs- The ones who give to others. They risk their lives for the sake of others, though their selflessness can often be replaced with recklessness.<br /> \u2022Kindness-Monday's Court, Gabriel's Court, the Azure Court, the Trumpet and Lily, Court of Heralds- They who feel the pain of others. Scholars and Messengers that seek to spread universal understanding, though they may find themselves sympathetic to the wrong sort. <br /> \u2022Clarity- Tuesday's Court, Raphael's Court, the Pool and the Fish, Court of Shepards- They who see themselves clearly. Healers and slayers of demons metaphorical and literal, they help others come to terms with themselves, even if they let themselves slip in the process.<br /> \u2022Faith- Wednesday's Court, Uriel's Court, the Scroll and the Flame, Court of Revealers- Those touched with a burning Faith. They tear away the falsehoods and impose the righteous order, even as their crusade burns away their humanity.<br /> \u2022Temperance- Thursday's Court, Cassiel's Court, the Eye and the Amulet, Court of Judges- Those who are truly impartial, caring only for justice. The Judges are notorious for being incorruptible and unswayed by any temptation, even as their judging eye falls on those who they find guilty. <br /> \u2022Diligence- Friday's Court, Jegudiel's Court, the Crown and the Whip, Court of Taskmasters- Those who work to completion for completion's sake. Foreman, laborers, and everything in-between. Though they put impossible expectations on others, it is obvious they never demand anything they do not plan to do themselves.<br /> \u2022Patience- Saturday's Court, Barachiel's Court, the Rose and the Storm, Court of Planners-Those who withhold their hand until the perfect moment. Well meant schemers, they set into motion massive undertakings others notice only when it's too late.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <b>COURTS OF THE MAGIC KINGDOM</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">The Magic Kingdom Freehold is based in Orlando, though it's not the only one there. The Changelings of this Freehold were inspired by the park that gives the Freehold its name. While I'm not quite sure what the proper story itself is, the big theme of it, the thing that defines the Freehold, is the idea of &quot;Disney Magic&quot;- that is, the idea that virtue is rewarded, that villains never really win, and that, while times may grow hard and things may look bleak, so long as you follow your dreams and stick to your principles, there will always be a happy ending. The Lost know that this isn't always or even mostly true, but they know the sheer power this belief holds, and channel it into a shield against their Keepers. Not sure about the Bulwark; I'm leaning towards a stage show or parade of some sort, maybe something focusing on the park itself, but I'm not quite sure.<br /> The Courts are:<br /> \u2022Tomorrowland(Wonder): Court of Innovation, Chrome Court, Court of Wonder, Tomorrow's Children, Star Seekers, Wonder Workers, Court of the Mad(derogatory)- While all the Courts have pledged themselves to the concept of &quot;Disney Magic&quot;, it is the Chrome Court that most eagerly embraced it. Though Tomorrow's Children were wounded by their Durance like everyone else, it also changed their world into something new and strange and oh so <i><b>shiny!</b></i> Similar in expertise to Autumn but very different in approach, the Star Seekers attempt to bring about the future into the now, even if they have to drag it kicking and screaming. <br /> \u2022Adventureland(Thrill): Court of Risks, Steel Court, Court of Explorers, Daring Discoverers, Outlanders, Thrill Seekers, Court of Fools(derogatory)- For the Court of Risks, everyday is a new adventure. Arcadia stole their freedom and left them caged; now they take it back with every new discovery and expedition, taming the world that hurt them with two fisted excellence and exotic grace. Lantern-jawed fighters, questing scholars, and bon vivants alike find their home amongst the Daring Discoverers. <br /> \u2022Main Street, U.S.A.(Comfort): Court of Home, Court of the Hearth, Court of Comfort, the Familiar Faces, the Homelanders, Comforting Souls, Court of Cowards(derogatory)- The Court of the Hearth lost much when they were stolen. They lost their lives, their safety, their blessed ignorance. They lost their <i>homes.</i> But now they've returned, and they refuse to lose it again. What unites the members of the Homelanders is the simple desire to protect people from the darkness, to let them stay ignorant for just a little longer. From the neighborhood watchman who does his duties with a smile and a Cold Iron knife to the housewife who's &quot;superstitions&quot; make the suburb shockingly resistant to Fey intrusion, their homes will not be violated. Never again. <br /> \u2022Liberty Square(Remembrance): Court of Reverence, Court of Stone, Court of Memory, Speakers of the Past, the Reminding Regiment, Court of the Hidebound(derogatory)-The Court of Reverence has always been prone to looking back. The past, a number will chime, was a better place, no matter if they mean in general or if just want to go back to their mortal days. Never the less, the Reminding Regiment is not to be discounted due to their backward gaze; the Court of Stone <i>remembers</i>, and the freehold is made stronger through it. By looking to what was, the Court shows the Lost how they have failed and how they might do better. <br /> \u2022Frontierland(Self-Sufficiency): Court of Independence, Court of Dust, Court of Survivors, Court of the Frontier, the Enduring Explorers, the Unbroken, Court of the Courtless(derogatory)- When looking back, the Unbroken blame only themselves. They were weak, or soft, or forcing others to do things for them, and that left them unable to fight back. Now the members of the Court of Dust refuse to be weak ever again, forging themselves into someone new. Members do acknowledge no man is an island, but to them it is just as much about protecting others as themselves; they refuse to let down others, refuse to be the weak link in the chain. And when they see the Huntsman again, they'll make sure it knows just how much they've grown.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> So, what do you all think?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-08T15:02:19-08:00","id":804036,"title":"","content":"<br /> <b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Portland Privateers</span></b><br /> After the fall of the previous Seasonal Freehold of Dormant Fire back in May 1980, from betrayal of cruel privateers that infiltrated the previous Freehold and held a mutiny which has come to shape the new Freehold of Portland Privateers, sometimes called Shanghai Stumptown, the Portland Metro area south of the Columbia river has been ruled by those who sacrifice others to keep the True Fae at bay while they revel free of most moral qualms. It can be said that all three courts with Portland Privateers focus on different aspects of getting others to comply with their desires, each in ways that are uniquely cruel. The massive goblin market sells slaves to the Gentry during the Portland Saturday Market down by the Ankeny Arches and Skidmore Fountain, of which the Monarchs of the three courts have their own stalls! The freeholds of the surrounding areas are populated by Lost who were once mortals abducted by Privateers in Portland and the hatred runs deep, but the membership of Shanghai Stumptown use every fair and foul trick to empower themselves and undercut any potentially successful attempts to strike back. Huntsmen from all over the region come to the Goblin Market that takes place during the Portland Saturday Market to buy assistance from the Portland Privateers which is gladly sold for tokens of Reprieve and the opportunity to send those that oppose them back to Arcadia. In fact if Portlanders get wind that a specific changeling has a Huntsmen on their trail, the Portlanders have been known to abduct the changeling first so they can use the powerful bargains protecting the Goblin Market to do the trade for a token of reprieve in relative safety. Beyond all these measures to secure their own safety at the cost of others, the bulwark protecting the Portland changelings from the machinations of the True Fae is fueled by each member contributing a mortal hostage to Arcadia, in exchange for their own safety and a fetch slave version of the person they send to Arcadia. Violence is almost unthinkable in Portland due to the gentrification of the city and the law enforcement is known for having the most police shootings of unarmed citizens in North America. Those changelings that have marched with violence in their heart against Portlanders seem to simply disappear without even a violent incident hitting the news and changelings around the Pacific Northwest live in dread of the whispers of what happens to those that cross a Portlander. Tales of all a person's loved ones being abducted, degraded, and violated in the most horrible ways imaginable and traded into Arcadian slavery, and their fetches used to stage mind-bending tragedies against those that are even rumored for the most minor offenses are enough to terrify most Lost from mere saying anything defiant against the Freehold. Worse than that, some of those that were thus abducted escape and have their minds twisted to enjoy the re-enacting their abuse on others and as willing masochists within the Portland Privateers.<br /> <br /> The Hedge in the Portland metro area is particularly miserable and ill suited for either travel or battle. There are very well worn trods but the traffic of Portlanders, Huntsmen, and True Fae make the main roads an extremely short trip back to Arcadia. The less traveled paths all wade through deep puddles and sometime pools of waist deep water, with sucking mud, narrow switchback paths, frequent dead ends, and quagmires that will swallow travelers whole without warning. Even the hobs and other strange creature that inhabit the Hedge around Portland seem especially cruel to those that are not part of the Portland Privateers. Those older changelings from around the Pacific Northwest that remember the time before the betrayal say in very hushed tones that it wasn't always as bad as it is now, and the cruel nature of the Freehold has warped Hedge into a viscous reflection of their vile hearts.<br /> <br /> <b>Seduction</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Spring has been replaced by The Court of Seduction, The wench's drugged tankard, (Heart-string's) Scissors<br /> Which rules the emotion: <i>Trust</i><br /> <br /> <b>Symbols:</b>Collars, White and Red, Broken Hearts and Upside-Down Tankards<br /> <br /> <b>Court Aspiration: </b>To use the trust of other as a weapon against them and never be vulnerable to trust again (Homebrew rule for <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\">Frailty Chronicles</a>)<br /> <br /> You'd think a court that masters trust would be somehow less cruel and petty, and this court would prove that is not necessarily the case. Mastering trust within themselves and other they work to endeavor they are always the ones betraying and not the ones betrayed, and are seemingly thwarted by the very hands of fate for their hubris.<br /> <br /> <b>Dot Values</b><br /> 1-Dot: -1 penalty to detect deception against the character<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow merit related to their court. (Examples: Types of Alcohol, Taverns, Sex, Etc...)<br /> 3-Dot: Sympathetic (\u2022\u2022)<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: True Friend (\u2022\u2022\u2022)<br /> <br /> <b>Approaches:</b><br /> &quot;One good turn deserves another&quot;, When a Huntsman is about to work their plot against a changeling of this court through in intermediary that was betrayed by the changeling, they gain a point of yearning. </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <b>Helplessness</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Winter has been replaced by The Court of Helplessness, The Bottomless Brig, Darkest Oubliette, (Eviction/Divorce/Subpoena) Papers<br /> Which rules the emotion: <i>Despair</i><br /> <br /> <b>Symbols:</b> Manacles, Black and Blue, Raining Clouds and Shut Iron-Bar Cage<br /> <br /> <b>Court Aspiration:</b> Crush the hopes of others as my hopes have been crushed. (Homebrew rule for <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\">Frailty Chronicles</a>)<br /> <br /> Not everyone in the Portland Privateer Freehold is happy about the sacrifices they have to make to the True Fae. Happy about their choices or not they never the less do it, and don't seem moved to recant anytime soon.<br /> <br /> <b>Dot Values</b><br /> 1-Dot: -1 penalty to motivate the character towards an action<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow merit related to their court.(Examples: Jail Cells, Handcuffs, Attempted Suicide, Drizzling Weather,Etc...)<br /> 3-Dot: Cursed (\u2022\u2022)<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot: Safe Place (\u2022\u2022\u2022)<br /> <br /> <b>Approaches:</b><br /> &quot;Misery loves company and when you cry, you cry alone&quot; When a huntsman is able to isolate a member of this court from their support network, they gain a point of Yearning. </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <b>Extortion</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Autumn and Summer have been replaced by The Court of Extortion, The Captain's Daughter, Rock (Upside the Head)<br /> Which rules the emotion: <i>Submission</i><br /> <br /> Why wield fear or anger against your enemy when they can provoke counter attacks? The emotion under fear and anger that causes people to bow down when pressure is applied is just as deeply buried in the heart, even though it is more refined and specific.<br /> <br /> <b>Symbols: </b>Whip, Silver and Gold, Scourge and Ship's Mast<br /> <br /> <b>Court Aspiration:</b> Never bend knee again while dominating all my lessors. (Homebrew rule for <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\">Frailty Chronicles</a>)<br /> <br /> <b>Dot Values</b><br /> 1-Dot: +1 Intimidation<br /> 2-Dot: Goblin Vow merit related to their court. (Examples: Torture Chambers, Flogs, Hostage Taking, Etc...)<br /> 3-Dot: Indomitable (\u2022\u2022)<br /> 4-Dot: Personal Approach<br /> 5-Dot:Staff (\u2022\u2022\u2022)<br /> <br /> Want people to lay down their lives and submit? Find out what they care about, and let them know it will never be safe unless they do as you say.<br /> <br /> <b>Approaches:</b><br /> &quot;Why am I surrounded by fools!&quot; When a Huntsman is about to use the incompetence of a changeling of this court's underlings against that changeling, they gain a point of Yearning. </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i><b>And the leaders of the freehold are real monsters...</b></i></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/895348-odd-market-thought?p=898280#post898280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Referencing the post linked in Quote^</div> </div> </div> </div>Adding these Horror Traits to Captains of <i>Extortion</i>, <i>Seduction</i>, and <i>Hopelessness </i>because they sold their mortality for Fae Letters of the Marque and Reprisal granting them Privateer rights under certain conditions<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling Horror:</b> Privateer Captain<br /> <br /> <b>Glamorous Will: </b>May spend Glamour instead of Willpower to use Dread Powers, and may spend Willpower instead of Glamour to use Contracts. This does not grant a Glamour Pool, which must be acquired from another source for this to be applicable for the character.<br /> <b>Potency: </b>The character gains one dot of Potency as if he were a Horror, this affect Willpower Capacity, and Supernatural Tolerance. For those with a Major Template the Supernatural Tolerance does stack with that granted by the splat\u2019s Power Stat.<br /> <b>Addicted to Privateering: </b>Every month a Privateer Captain goes without fulfilling their feeding Aspiration the lose access to a Willpower Dot, but upon fulfilling the Privateer Captain\u2019s Feeding Aspiration he regains access to all the Willpower Dots they lost access to, in the state they were lost in(filled with Willpower Points or empty). Privateer Captains also gain Willpower points by fulfilling their Feeding Aspiration. Unlike other types of Aspirations the Feeding Aspiration does not grant Beats.<br /> <b>Feeding Aspiration: </b>To use their <b>Miracle </b>Dread Power as described.<br /> <br /> <b>Dread Powers:</b><br /> <b>Immortal</b> (Bane of having their wax candle figurine representing their mortality harmed, -Lighting the candle caused the figurine and the person represented to age instead of burn down normally-)<br /> <b>Unbreakable</b><br /> <b>Miracle</b>-By acting as a middle-man in a bargain selling a minor or non-template to the Hob holding their mortality to cause a minor or non-template to become a changeling thus making a fetch, which is enslaved to the person selling to the aforementioned Hob. Must have Fae Letters of the Marque and Reprisal granting them Privateer rights which was bought with their mortality on their person to make the deal.<br /> <br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Please note Human Trafficking is a real life problem in Portland Oregon, both historically and to this day, and it features heavily as a theme and strongly affects motifs of Chronicles of Darkness I run based around Portland. For more information about this real life problem please see the non-profit organization page trying to help: <a href=\"http://www.adornedingrace.org/\" target=\"_blank\">link</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 05-25-2016, 05:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-15T00:57:32-07:00","id":847575,"title":"","content":"I hope that I haven't committed the Forum Sin of &quot;Necroing&quot; this thread, but I have been really excited for the court creation and Freehold creation rules. I've enjoyed reading everyone's replies here and I think that so many people have some really good ideas.<br /> <br /> For the last two months or so, I've been writing the background for the game I will run in Changeling 2.0 and I wanted to share what I have with you for questions, comments, and concerns.<br /> <br /> Here is the link to the Google Document: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1KijHFNd-CNX7bZx2WI4BBkkpz8hZ2IOTHSvhn5H8MW8/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://docs.google.com/document/d/1...it?usp=sharing</a><br /> <br /> It's about 20 pages long, so the TL;DR version is as such: The Shenandoah Valley of VA has been invaded countless of times in the first 100 years of American History. The Civil War caused the old Freehold's bulwark to break, and the new Freehold formed around the story of Stonewall Jackson, a local hero. The Freehold's bulwark is to defend this watchtower taken from the Great Wall of China/Lord of the Rings, which if there is another invasion, they light to warn the other freeholds in the area. The Castellan of the Freehold is chosen each year as the person who scouts out the most likely form of danger/threat to the Freehold from the hedge. Each month, motley's are drafted to spend that month in the hedge at the tower. There are Three courts, named after the different forms of transportation that are used within the valley. The Courts of Iron, Stone, and the Rapids. They each are situated in an area and run the area similar to the directional courts, each has a leader and as you move between the freehold the areas of jurisdiction change.<br /> <br /> The Iron court is named after the Railroad system that has been in the Valley for a long time. They are the court of Pride. The Stone court is named after the highway system, and they claim Tenacity as their emotion of choice. The Court of the Rapids comes from the river transportation and their emotion is Faith. I chose these three emotions because they are very exemplary of the location that the game is set in, imagine a rural Appalachia/Mountain Valley, in Virginia.<br /> <br /> I have used a lot of quotations from Stonewall Jackson himself, and I apologize if this offends anyone. I have not used any aspect of the Confederate States of America that include slavery, nor do I condone it. I just thought the legends of his heroics as well as him being from the area really would have energized a newly emerging Freehold.<br /> <br /> Please, let me know what you think! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11332,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11332-freresebastian","user":"FrereSebastian","date":". 03-15-2016, 04:08 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11332-freresebastian","name":"FrereSebastian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-15T14:02:47-07:00","id":847850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>FrereSebastian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=847575#post847575\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I hope that I haven't committed the Forum Sin of &amp;quot;Necroing&amp;quot; this thread, but I have been really excited for the court creation and Freehold creation rules. I've enjoyed reading everyone's replies here and I think that so many people have some really good ideas.<br /> <br /> For the last two months or so, I've been writing the background for the game I will run in Changeling 2.0 and I wanted to share what I have with you for questions, comments, and concerns.<br /> <br /> Here is the link to the Google Document: <a href=\"https://&amp;quot;https://docs.google.com/document/d/1KijHFNd-CNX7bZx2WI4BBkkpz8hZ2IOTHSvhn5H8MW8/edit?usp=sharing&amp;quot;\" target=\"_blank\">https://docs.google.com/document/d/1...it?usp=sharing</a><br /> <br /> It's about 20 pages long, so the TL;DR version is as such: The Shenandoah Valley of VA has been invaded countless of times in the first 100 years of American History. The Civil War caused the old Freehold's bulwark to break, and the new Freehold formed around the story of Stonewall Jackson, a local hero. The Freehold's bulwark is to defend this watchtower taken from the Great Wall of China/Lord of the Rings, which if there is another invasion, they light to warn the other freeholds in the area. The Castellan of the Freehold is chosen each year as the person who scouts out the most likely form of danger/threat to the Freehold from the hedge. Each month, motley's are drafted to spend that month in the hedge at the tower. There are Three courts, named after the different forms of transportation that are used within the valley. The Courts of Iron, Stone, and the Rapids. They each are situated in an area and run the area similar to the directional courts, each has a leader and as you move between the freehold the areas of jurisdiction change.<br /> <br /> The Iron court is named after the Railroad system that has been in the Valley for a long time. They are the court of Pride. The Stone court is named after the highway system, and they claim Tenacity as their emotion of choice. The Court of the Rapids comes from the river transportation and their emotion is Faith. I chose these three emotions because they are very exemplary of the location that the game is set in, imagine a rural Appalachia/Mountain Valley, in Virginia.<br /> <br /> I have used a lot of quotations from Stonewall Jackson himself, and I apologize if this offends anyone. I have not used any aspect of the Confederate States of America that include slavery, nor do I condone it. I just thought the legends of his heroics as well as him being from the area really would have energized a newly emerging Freehold.<br /> <br /> Please, let me know what you think! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Reading through the document, at the moment. And so far, I'm liking what's been written down, so far. Will have more to say when I'm done.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, I'm trying to put together a Court System that can be usable in a Filipino setting, which draws from folklore surrounding indigenuous pre-colonial animistic religions.<br /> <br /> In fact, I had a very radical approach to it. What if the Courts based on that system honored (exiled) True Fae as part of their Bulwark? Frankly speaking, I can see the Diwatas working as Gentry, though I still am working out the details for how they could differ from Others elsewhere. Luckily, the 1E homebrew supplement, the Goblin Chronicles, gives me something to work with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/806810-deionscribe-s-homebrew-hub#post806810\" target=\"_blank\">&quot;My Homebrew Hub&quot;</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/828132-open-invite-age-of-azar\" target=\"_blank\">Age of Azar</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/907224-the-kingdom-of-yamatai-dark-era\" target=\"_blank\">The Kingdom of Yamatai</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8180-deionscribe","name":"Deionscribe","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-06T07:04:33-07:00","id":982673,"title":"","content":"Hi! I'm sorry for raising this thread again from the grave, but I haven't seen this answered anywhere else: What are the sample settings that are going to appear in the corebook? I know that Toronto, Tokyo and Budapest are going to be in, but what else?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-06T07:10:03-07:00","id":982676,"title":"","content":"The final set is Toronto, Miami, Tokyo, and Paris.<br /> <br /> Budapest unfortunately had to be dropped due to scheduling conflicts, as did Cape Town.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-06T11:16:53-07:00","id":982810,"title":"","content":"Thank you! It's a pity I don't get to see Budapest in the core book, though I still have high hopes for reading about the setting in a not-so-future supplement.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-06T11:40:38-07:00","id":982820,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=982676#post982676\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The final set is Toronto, Miami, Tokyo, and Paris.<br /> <br /> Budapest unfortunately had to be dropped due to scheduling conflicts, as did Cape Town.</div> </div> </div> </div>oh- that's unfortunate. What I love the most about the new 2e books is the example settings, ans as many as possible. Still, hope to see those (and others perhaps) in future supplements, as Zooroos has said.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-06T12:25:20-07:00","id":982849,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zooroos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/551489-court-creation?p=982810#post982810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thank you! It's a pity I don't get to see Budapest in the core book, though I still have high hopes for reading about the setting in a not-so-future supplement.</div> </div> </div> </div>This, though I would have been most interested in Cape Town myself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-06T14:53:21-07:00","id":982943,"title":"","content":"I really wanted Budapest and Cape Town as well, and I hope we'll see them in future too.<br /> <br /> I mean, pleased as punch I got to contribute to the core with Paris late, but Budapest and Cape Town were going to be awesome.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-07-18T12:30:47-07:00","id":1117885,"title":"","content":"<i>Updated due to:</i> <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/tumbledown-market-changeling-the-lost/\" target=\"_blank\">Tumbledown Market Preview</a><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-size:24px\">Portland Privateers</span></b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">After the fall of the previous Seasonal Freehold of Dormant Fire back in May 1980, from betrayal of cruel privateers that infiltrated the previous Freehold and held a mutiny which has come to shape the new Freehold of Portland Privateers, sometimes called Shanghai Stumptown, the Portland Metro area south of the Columbia river has been ruled by those who sacrifice others to keep the True Fae at bay while they revel free of most moral qualms. It can be said that all three courts with Portland Privateers focus on different aspects of getting others to comply with their desires, each in ways that are uniquely cruel. The massive goblin market sells slaves to the Gentry during the Portland Saturday Market down by the Ankeny Arches and Skidmore Fountain, of which the Monarchs of the three courts have their own slave selling stalls! The Portland Goblin Market is a particularly shady section of the Tumbledown Market which only connects on the days the mundane market is open. The freeholds of the surrounding areas are populated by Lost who were once mortals abducted by Privateers in Portland and the hatred runs deep, but the membership of Shanghai Stumptown use every fair and foul trick to empower themselves and undercut any potentially successful attempts to strike back. Huntsmen from all over the region come to the Goblin Market that takes place during the Portland Saturday Market to buy assistance from the Portland Privateers which is gladly sold for tokens of Reprieve and the opportunity to send those that oppose them back to Arcadia. In fact if Portlanders get wind that a specific changeling has a Huntsmen on their trail, the Portlanders have been known to abduct the changeling first so they can use the powerful bargains protecting the Goblin Market to do the trade for a token of reprieve in relative safety. Beyond all these measures to secure their own safety at the cost of others, the bulwark protecting the Portland changelings from the machinations of the True Fae is fueled by each member contributing a mortal hostage to Arcadia, in exchange for their own safety and a fetch slave version of the person they send to Arcadia.<br /> <br /> Violence is almost unthinkable in Portland due to the gentrification of the city and the law enforcement is known for having the some of the most police shootings of unarmed citizens in North America. Those changelings that have marched with violence in their heart against Portlanders seem to simply disappear without even a violent incident hitting the news and changelings around the Pacific Northwest live in dread of the whispers of what happens to those that cross a Portlander. Tales of all a person's loved ones being abducted, degraded, and violated in the most horrible ways imaginable and traded into Arcadian slavery, and their fetches used to stage mind-bending tragedies against those that are even rumored for the most minor offenses are enough to terrify most Lost from mere saying anything defiant against the Freehold. Worse than that, some of those that were thus abducted escape and have their minds twisted to enjoy the re-enacting their abuse on others and as willing masochists within the Portland Privateers.<br /> <br /> The Hedge in the Portland metro area is particularly miserable and ill suited for either travel or battle. There are very well worn trods but the traffic of Portlanders, Huntsmen, and True Fae make the main roads an extremely short trip back to Arcadia. The less traveled paths all wade through deep puddles and sometime pools of waist deep water, with sucking mud, narrow switchback paths, frequent dead ends, and quagmires that will swallow travelers whole without warning. Even the hobs and other strange creature that inhabit the Hedge around Portland seem especially cruel to those that are not part of the Portland Privateers. Those older changelings from around the Pacific Northwest that remember the time before the betrayal say in very hushed tones that it wasn't always as bad as it is now, and the cruel nature of the Freehold has warped Hedge into a viscous reflection of their vile hearts.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>Seduction</b></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Spring has been replaced by The Court of Seduction, The wench's drugged tankard, (Heart-string's) Scissors<br /> <b>Which rules the emotion:</b> Trust<br /> <br /> <b>Symbols:</b>Collars, White and Red, Broken Hearts and Upside-Down Tankards<br /> <br /> <b>Court Aspiration:</b> To use the trust of other as a weapon against them and never be vulnerable to trust again (Homebrew rule for Frailty Chronicles)<br /> <br /> You'd think a court that masters trust would be somehow less cruel and petty, and this court would prove that is not necessarily the case. Mastering trust within themselves and others they endeavor that they are always the ones betraying and not the ones betrayed, and are seemingly thwarted by the very hands of fate for their hubris.<br /> <br /> <b>Harvest: </b>Your character gains a Glamour point whenever she gets someone to eagerly put themselves in a vulnerable position regarding her.<br /> <br /> <b>Dot Values</b><br /> \u2022 Gain bonus dice equal to your character\u2019s Mantle dots on mundane rolls to figure out who someone trusts.<br /> \u2022\u2022 Gain bonus dice equal to your character\u2019s Mantle dots on mundane rolls to persuade to trust you.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per chapter, you may spend a Willpower point to grant a number equal to your Mantle dots of your character's social merits which represent relationships the benefit of the True Friend Merit, to a maximum of five dots, for the scene.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per chapter may spend a Willpower to ignore a number of Doors when only using Soft Leverage<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per chapter, you may spend a Willpower point to improve your Impression with someone to Perfect for a scene.<br /> <br /> <b>Approaches:</b><br /> &quot;One good turn deserves another&quot;, When a Huntsman is about to work their plot against a changeling of this court through in intermediary that was betrayed by the changeling, they gain a point of yearning. </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>Helplessness</b></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Winter has been replaced by The Court of Helplessness, The Bottomless Brig, Darkest Oubliette, (Eviction/Divorce/Subpoena) Papers<br /> Which rules the emotion: Despair<br /> <br /> <b>Symbols:</b> Manacles, Black and Blue, Raining Clouds and Shut Iron-Bar Cage<br /> <br /> <b>Court Aspiration:</b> Crush the hopes of others as my hopes have been crushed. (Homebrew rule for Frailty Chronicles)<br /> <br /> Not everyone in the Portland Privateer Freehold is happy about the sacrifices they have to make to the True Fae. Happy about their choices or not they never the less do it, and don't seem moved to recant anytime soon.<br /> <br /> <b>Harvest:</b> Your character gains a Glamour point whenever he successfully makes someone's hope unreachable.<br /> <br /> <b>Dot Values</b><br /> \u2022 Gain bonus dice equal to your character\u2019s Mantle dots on mundane rolls to ignore Hard Leverage to get you to act.<br /> \u2022\u2022 Gain Doors needing to be opened equal to your character\u2019s Mantle dots when Hard Leverage is used against you.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 Other characters take a dice penalty equal to your character\u2019s Mantle dots to mundane rolls to force or intimidate you to action.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per chapter regain all Willpower when either something that would be wonderful for you falls through or something awful happens to you or those you care about.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per scene, you may spend a Willpower point to learn what is currently considered the greatest tragedy experienced by any character present.<br /> <br /> <b>Approaches:</b><br /> &quot;Misery loves company and when you cry, you cry alone&quot; When a huntsman is able to isolate a member of this court from their support network, they gain a point of Yearning. </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>Extortion</b></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Autumn and Summer have been replaced by The Court of Extortion, The Captain's Daughter, Rock (Upside the Head)<br /> <b>Which rules the emotion:</b> Submission<br /> <br /> Why wield fear or anger against your enemy when they can provoke counter attacks? The emotion under fear and anger that causes people to bow down when pressure is applied is just as deeply buried in the heart, even though it is more refined and specific.<br /> <br /> <b>Symbols: </b>Whip, Silver and Gold, Scourge and Ship's Mast<br /> <br /> <b>Court Aspiration: </b>Never bend knee again while dominating all my lessors. (Homebrew rule for Frailty Chronicles)<br /> <br /> <b>Harvest: </b>Your character gains a Glamour point whenever she successfully makes another bend to your character's will.<br /> <br /> <b>Dot Values</b><br /> \u2022 Gain bonus dice equal to your character\u2019s Mantle dots on mundane rolls to dominate and command others through force of personality.<br /> \u2022\u2022 Gain bonus dice equal to your character\u2019s Mantle dots on mundane rolls to intimidate those that don't submit willingly.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per chapter, you may spend a Willpower point to grant your character additional dots of the Staff Merit equal to her Mantle dots, to a maximum of five, for the scene.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per chapter, you may spend a Willpower to reassign a Leverage Condition on someone else whose leveraged by someone whom you hold the Leverage Condition over to yourself instead.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 Once per scene, you may spend a Willpower to force a character to resolve a Leveraged Condition in relation to you if you can communicate with them.<br /> <br /> Want people to lay down their lives and submit? Find out what they care about, and let them know it will never be safe unless they do as you say.<br /> <br /> <b>Approaches:</b><br /> &quot;Why am I surrounded by fools!&quot; When a Huntsman is about to use the incompetence of a changeling of this court's underlings against that changeling, they gain a point of Yearning. </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> And the leaders of the freehold are real monsters...<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/895348-odd-market-thought?p=898280#post898280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Referencing the post linked in Quote^</div> </div> </div> </div>Adding these Horror Traits to Captains of Extortion, Seduction, and Hopelessness because they sold their mortality for Fae Letters of the Marque and Reprisal granting them Privateer rights under certain conditions<br /> <br /> Changeling Horror: Privateer Captain<br /> <br /> Glamorous Will: May spend Glamour instead of Willpower to use Dread Powers, and may spend Willpower instead of Glamour to use Contracts. This does not grant a Glamour Pool, which must be acquired from another source for this to be applicable for the character.<br /> Potency: The character gains one dot of Potency as if he were a Horror, this affect Willpower Capacity, and Supernatural Tolerance. For those with a Major Template the Supernatural Tolerance does stack with that granted by the splat\u2019s Power Stat.<br /> Addicted to Privateering: Every month a Privateer Captain goes without fulfilling their feeding Aspiration the lose access to a Willpower Dot, but upon fulfilling the Privateer Captain\u2019s Feeding Aspiration he regains access to all the Willpower Dots they lost access to, in the state they were lost in(filled with Willpower Points or empty). Privateer Captains also gain Willpower points by fulfilling their Feeding Aspiration. Unlike other types of Aspirations the Feeding Aspiration does not grant Beats.<br /> Feeding Aspiration: To use their Miracle Dread Power as described.<br /> <br /> Dread Powers:<br /> Immortal (Bane of having their wax candle figurine representing their mortality harmed, -Lighting the candle caused the figurine and the person represented to age instead of burn down normally-)<br /> Unbreakable<br /> Miracle-By acting as a middle-man in a bargain selling a minor or non-template to the Hob holding their mortality to cause a minor or non-template to become a changeling thus making a fetch, which is enslaved to the person selling to the aforementioned Hob. Must have Fae Letters of the Marque and Reprisal granting them Privateer rights which was bought with their mortality on their person to make the deal.<br /> <br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Please note Human Trafficking is a real life problem in Portland Oregon, both historically and to this day, and it features heavily as a theme and strongly affects motifs of Chronicles of Darkness I run based around Portland. For more information about this real life problem please see the non-profit organization page trying to help: link","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 07-18-2017, 04:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}